{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-af6c8976e231e212","type":"tool_result","content":"100\tand SDH subtitles, which include salient non-verbal audio for hard-of-hearing audiences.\n101\tMoreover, we adapt existing evaluation metrics to our novel AD task and benchmark\n102\tcurrent AD generation systems and LLMs under this formulation. Our contributions are:\n103\t• A realistic task formulation for AD generation (a model must decide when and\n104\twhat to describe), together with evaluation metrics that exploit multiple references.\n105\t• Re frame d, a dataset of 2,023 video excerpts (3,302 scenes) from 206 movies with\n106\tdual AD versions (US and UK), dialogue subtitles, SDH, screenplays, and video.\n107\t• The Re frame d challenge set of 10 fully manually annotated movies with 2–3\n108\tprofessionally transcribed AD versions each, scene boundaries, AD-to-scene labels,\n109\tscreenplay alignment, and professional subtitles.\n110\t• A suite of benchmarks for ASR quality, temporal alignment, screenplay alignment,\n111\tscene segmentation, AD splitting, and AD generation on the above data.\n112\t\n113\t2\n114\t\n115\t\fPublished as a conference paper at COLM 2026\n116\t\n117\tSCENE 1\n118\t\n119\tSCENE 25\n120\t\n121\t...\n122\t\n123\tSCENE 50\n124\t\n125\t...\n126\t\n127\tSCENE 75\n128\t\n129\t...\n130\t\n131\tSCENE 100\n132\t\n133\t...\n134\t\n135\tSCENE 120\n136\t\n137\tAligned\n138\tscreenplay\n139\t\n140\t...\n141\t\n142\tVideo\n143\texcerpts\n144\t\n145\t...\n146\t\n147\t...\n148\t\n149\t...\n150\t\n151\t[MIKAEL]\n152\tWe’re late.\n153\t\n154\t?\n155\t\n156\t[ERIKA]\n157\tFashionably\n158\tlate.\n159\t\n160\t?\n161\t\n162\t?\n163\t\n164\t...\n165\t\n166\tDialogue\n167\tsubtitles\n168\t\n169\tShe watches numbly as the couple walk\n170\taway with their arms around each other.\n171\t\n172\t...\n173\t\n174\tAD\n175\t\n176\tAD Generation\n177\tShe takes a package from the\n178\t\n179\t. . . back of the bike, but stops dead\n180\twhen she sees Mikael leave his\n181\tapartment with Erika.\n182\t\n183\tDialogue\n184\t\n185\tDialogue gap\n186\t\n187\tAD\n188\t\n189\tNo AD\n190\t\n191\tFigure 2: The Re frame d task formulation. Given input of a video, dialogue subtitles,\n192\tand the spans of gaps between them, a model must decide both when and what to describe.\n193\tNarrative context can be made available from the movie’s screenplay and other scene videos.\n194\t\n195\t2\n196\t\n197\tRelated Work\n198\t\n199\tAD as Video Captioning. The task has commonly been framed as a form of video captioning:\n200\tgiven a short video clip, generate a natural language sentence that describes its visually\n201\tsalient content. Most current work follows this formulation and finetunes multimodal\n202\tmodels for the task, augmenting input with additional signals such as broader visual\n203\tcontext, character information, or scene-level cues (Wang et al., 2021; Han et al., 2023a;b;\n204\t2024; Lin et al., 2024; Lee et al., 2025; Deganutti et al., 2025; Wang et al., 2025; Ye et al.,\n205\t2025). DistinctAD (Fang et al., 2025) trains using character information, visual features and\n206\ttext from nearby clips, with architectural components and learning objectives that reward\n207\tdistinctive descriptions. Xie et al. (2024) propose to first generate a detailed description\n208\tof a clip and then summarize it into AD; in Shot-by-Shot (Xie et al., 2025), this approach\n209\tis improved by incorporating knowledge of shot boundaries and scale. Other work relies\n210\ton LLM prompting (Lin et al., 2023; Chu et al., 2024; Zhang et al., 2024; Ye et al., 2024;\n211\tKhandelwal et al., 2025); Gao et al. (2025) provide a broader survey of the area. A related\n212\tline of work incorporates screenplays as narrative context. Screenplays have been estimated\n213\tto contain approximately 60% of the information required for AD generation (Lakritz &\n214\tSalway, 2006), and later work shows that screenplay context can improve generation quality\n215\t(Rimle et al., 2020; Campos et al., 2023; Park et al., 2025). Despite this progress, all these\n216\tapproaches address a simplified formulation of AD creation: they assume AD placement\n217\tis given and only generate the description sentence. Our benchmark is the first to require\n218\tsystems to determine placement and content jointly.\n219\tMovie Datasets with AD. Many movie datasets exist without AD (Tapaswi et al., 2016;\n220\tGu et al., 2018; Vicol et al., 2018; Huang et al., 2020; Bain et al., 2020; Wu & Krahenbuhl,\n221\t2021; Sun et al., 2025; 2024; Wu et al., 2024; Zaranis et al., 2025). The first large-scale\n222\tdataset containing AD is LSMDC (Rohrbach et al., 2017), which pairs semi-automatically\n223\ttranscribed AD sentences with manually extracted movie clips that correspond with the\n224\tdescribed elements, replacing character names with placeholders. MAD (Soldan et al., 2022)\n225\tscales this up by fully automatically transcribing AD tracks and aligning them to full movies\n226\t3\n227\t\n228\t\fPublished as a conference paper at COLM 2026\n229\t\n230\tvia audio cross-correlation. Evaluation material reuses LSMDC timecoding, which is suited\n231\tto their task of video grounding. Han et al. (2023a) repurpose a subset of this evaluation\n232\tdata for a full-movie AD generation benchmark; the adoption of the LSMDC timecoding\n233\tmeans that the time-span of the element to be described in the generation is provided as\n234\tinput. The placeholder substitution for character names is also retained. Han et al. (2024)\n235\tintroduce CMD-AD by aligning AD segments to movie scenes from YouTube and allowing\n236\ttemporal offsets and speed adjustments to account for differences in broadcast frame rates\n237\t(e.g., 23.976fps NTSC vs. 25fps PAL).\n238\tThese datasets share three limitations. First, AD transcripts are produced automatically, yet\n239\ttranscription quality has never been evaluated; in Appendix C.1 we show this is a major\n240\tissue. Second, the alignment of AD tracks to movie scenes has also not been validated; in\n241\tAppendix C.2 we show that the method used for the construction of CMD-AD is very noisy.\n242\tThird, the MAD-based evaluation set inherits LSMDC timecoding, transcription errors and\n243\tcharacter name replacement, issues that have propagated into subsequent work.\n244\tEvaluation of AD Generation. Appendix Table 3 summarizes the many metrics used to\n245\tevaluate AD generation. They include n-gram (e.g. METEOR, Banerjee & Lavie, 2005),\n246\timage-captioning (e.g. CIDEr, Vedantam et al., 2015), and QA-based evaluation (Kala et al.,\n247\t2025). Dense video captioning metrics such as SODA (Fujita et al., 2020) and its temporal\n248\tcounterpart (Batra et al., 2022) align generated and reference elements using their time\n249\tspans before scoring them with F-measure. However, these metrics inherit limitations from\n250\tthe simplified task setting. Because prior work assumes fixed AD placement, evaluation\n251\toperates at the level of individual captions matched to predefined clips and therefore cannot\n252\tassess whether a system correctly decides when to insert a description as well as what visual\n253\telement needs to be described. Prior work also ignores the task’s subjectivity and typically\n254\tuses only a single reference. We adapt existing metrics to our open-ended task formulation,\n255\tand use multiple references throughout.\n256\t\n257\t3\n258\t\n259\tThe Re frame d Dataset\n260\t\n261\tWe now provide an overview of the Re frame d dataset and its construction, including\n262\tquantitative validation of each tool used (results are summarized in Section 3.2, with more\n263\tdetails in Appendix C and E). Our design is guided by the goal of supporting AD generation\n264\tas a joint decision problem over when and what to describe, informed by narrative context.\n265\t3.1\n266\t\n267\tData and Benchmark Overview\n268\t\n269\tThe dataset is built from movie excerpts licensed by Fandango, with raw videos without\n270\twatermarking available publicly on rottentomatoes.com. Narrative context is provided\n271\tfrom full-movie screenplays that we align to the movie timelines as well as other videos\n272\tfrom the same movie. We select movies with at least five movie excerpt videos and listings\n273\ton audiovault.net for both American and British AD versions. Table 1 compares our\n274\tdataset against publicly available alternatives, showing our contributions of providing more\n275\tparallel data streams for models to learn from, multiple AD references for both training and\n276\tevaluation material, and gold standard data for evaluation. Following prior work (Bamman\n277\tet al., 2024), we exclude animated movies as they represent a substantially different visual\n278\tdomain.\n279\tAD Transcription and Alignment. We obtain transcription for all AD tracks: for evaluation\n280\tmovies, they are the result of professional human transcription; training movies rely on a\n281\tSpeechmatics-based pipeline (Speechmatics are a specialist ASR and diarization provider).\n282\tFor each movie, we align the video excerpts to the full AD track (both American and British\n283\tversions) using cross-correlation, accounting for possible PAL–NTSC speed differences and\n284\tenforcing consistent alignment offsets across scenes from the same movie.\n285\tParallel Data Streams. For each movie excerpt, we provide professional English dialogueonly and SDH subtitles sourced from opensubtitles.org. They are aligned to video using\n286\tASR-based timestamp matching (Appendix G). For the 85 movies with parsed screenplays\n287\tavailable in MovieSum (Saxena & Keller, 2024), we align screenplay scenes to movie scenes\n288\t4\n289\t\n290\t\fPublished as a conference paper at COLM 2026\n291\t\n292\tTRAINING\n293\thrs #refs\n294\t\n295\tTESTING\n296\tTASK\n297\thrs #refs\n298\t\n299\tName\n300\t\n301\tType\n302\t\n303\tLSMDC (AD)\n304\tMAD\n305\tMAD-v2-eval\n306\tMAD-v3-eval\n307\tRe frame d\n308\t\n309\t5s clips\n310\tfull movies\n311\tfull movies\n312\tfull movies\n313\tfull movies\n314\t\n315\t✔ ✔ ✔ ✘\n316\t✘ ✘ ✘ ✘\n317\t– – – –\n318\t– – – –\n319\t– – – –\n320\t\n321\t✔ 134\n322\t✔ 990\n323\t–\n324\t–\n325\t–\n326\t–\n327\t–\n328\t–\n329\t\n330\t1\n331\t1\n332\t–\n333\t–\n334\t–\n335\t\n336\t✔ ✔ ✔ ✘ ✔ 12\n337\t✘ ✘ ✘ ✘ ✔ 217\n338\t✘ ✘ ✔ ✘ ✔ 19\n339\t✘ ✘ ✔ ✘ ✔ 19\n340\t✔ ✔ ✔ ✔ ✔ 21\n341\t\n342\t1\n343\t1\n344\t1\n345\t1\n346\t2-3\n347\t\n348\tVC\n349\tVG\n350\tVC\n351\tASR\n352\tAD\n353\t\n354\tCMD-AD\n355\tRe frame d\n356\t\n357\t2m scenes\n358\t2m scenes\n359\t\n360\t✔ ✔ ✔ ✘ ✔ 307\n361\t✔ ✔ ✔ ✔ ✔ 77\n362\t\n363\t1\n364\t2\n365\t\n366\t✔ ✔ ✔ ✘ ✔\n367\t✔ ✔ ✔ ✔ ✔\n368\t\n369\t22\n370\t4\n371\t\n372\t1\n373\t2\n374\t\n375\tVC\n376\tAD\n377\t\n378\tTable 1: Comparison of publicly available AD datasets. We report whether video ( ),\n379\taudio ( ), subtitles ( ), screenplays ( ), and AD transcripts ( ) are available at goldstandard quality (✔), silver-standard quality (✔; transcription or alignment), or not at all\n380\t(✘), alongside total hours of content and number of AD references. VC = video captioning;\n381\tVG = video grounding; ASR = automatic speech recognition (and diarization) of AD; AD =\n382\tour task of AD generation. Training includes validation data, if it is provided. CMD-AD\n383\thours are based on the available video data. Our contributions are highlighted.\n384\tusing Vecalign (Thompson & Koehn, 2019) applied to joint dialogue and AD representations,\n385\tenabling robust alignment despite script deviations.\n386\tAD Splitting and Scene Segmentation. We split AD transcripts into segments that each\n387\tcorrespond to one visual element. For all evaluation data, the splitting is a part of the\n388\tprofessional human transcription; for training data, it is from a learned splitting model.\n389\tWe additionally apply a second learned model that predicts scene boundaries from AD\n390\tsegments. Appendix K shows split elements for our earlier example.\n391\tDataset Splits. The final dataset contains 206 movies and 2,023 video excerpts (average\n392\tduration 144 seconds), spanning 3,302 scenes; 85 movies have aligned screenplays. We\n393\tsplit the data into training, validation, and test sets such that the genre distribution is\n394\tbalanced across the splits (Appendix I). The training set excludes movies appearing in\n395\tprevious evaluation sets. The validation set consists of 10 movies from existing evaluation\n396\tdatasets, with aligned screenplays, while the test set contains 10 recent movies (2023–2025)\n397\tnot present in prior datasets. Validation and test movie excerpts benefit from professionally\n398\ttranscribed AD rather than automatic transcription (Appendix B).\n399\tChallenge Benchmark. For full-movie evaluation, we provide a manually annotated\n400\tbenchmark of 10 movies, each with 2–3 AD versions, exact scene boundaries, per-element\n401\tscene labels, scene sequences, screenplay alignment, and professional dialogue and SDH\n402\tsubtitles (Appendix D). All annotations were performed manually, either by a professional\n403\tpost-production company or by a trained annotator. Seven of these movies are drawn from\n404\tMAD-v3-eval; we additionally include Dune: Part Two (2024), Barbie (2023), and Priscilla\n405\t(2023), selected for their award-winning AD tracks. The resulting set of ten movies spans\n406\t20 years of cinema and covers a diverse range of genres — 14 out of the 19 IMDb genre\n407\tcategories in the training set are represented.\n408\t3.2\n409\t\n410\tThe Construction Pipeline\n411\t\n412\tGold-standard Transcription. All downstream evaluation depends on gold-standard AD\n413\ttranscription, which is unavailable from existing data. We hence contracted a film postproduction company to perform fully manual AD transcription and timecoding to the\n414\tnearest frame (approximately 0.04 seconds), and to provide segments each corresponding\n415\tto a single visual element of the movie, which we will call description elements. We call this\n416\tnew resource MAD-v3-eval. Analysis of these annotations shows that AD narration occurs\n417\twithin 10 seconds of the described elements, but not necessarily at exactly the same time as\n418\tthem, reflecting the need to place AD within available dialogue gaps (Figure 3).\n419\tAD Transcription and Diarization. The first pipeline stage takes a full-length AD audio\n420\ttrack as input and produces a transcription with speaker labels and timestamps. We\n421\t5\n422\t\n423\t\fPublished as a conference paper at COLM 2026\n424\t\n425\tFigure 3: Histogram comparing (a) MADv2-eval timecoding (v2; when described\n426\telements occur) and (b) new professional\n427\ttimecoding (v3; when the AD is spoken).\n428\tTexts are normalized and aligned at the\n429\tword level, and v2 segments matching\n430\ta concatenation of one or more v3 segments are retained. We plot the midpoint\n431\tdifferences.\n432\t\n433\t300\n434\t\n435\t−10\n436\t\n437\t−5\n438\t\n439\t0\n440\t\n441\t5\n442\t\n443\t10\n444\t\n445\t∆ AD spoken vs. event occurred (secs)\n446\t\n447\tevaluate multiple ASR systems against MAD-v3-eval and find that existing AD corpora are\n448\textremely noisy (WhisperX achieves a best overall word error rate, WER=12.8; the MADv2-eval transcriptions achieve 22.1). A system based on Speechmatics with character-name\n449\tadaptation achieves WER=2.9 and is used to transcribe all training data (Appendix C.1).\n450\tTemporal Alignment. The second stage aligns scene videos to full-length AD tracks using\n451\tcross-correlation (Soldan et al., 2022), accounting for frame-rate differences between PAL\n452\tand NTSC formats (Appendix Figure 5). This approach is robust for typical scene durations\n453\t(100% alignment accuracy to within one frame for scenes of length 32 seconds or greater,\n454\twhich accounts for >99% of the video extracts in our data), and we enforce consistency of\n455\talignment offsets across scenes within each movie (Appendix C.2).\n456\tAD Splitting and Scene Segmentation. ASR produces sentence-level output, but our\n457\tevaluation data is segmented into description elements. We therefore segment transcribed\n458\tAD sentences using a RoBERTa-based model trained on gold-standard annotations (F1 =64.8\n459\tvs. a comma-splitting baseline F1 =36.9; Appendix E.3). A classifier over these segments then\n460\tidentifies scene boundaries, exploiting textual cues (e.g., “Now”) that AD guidelines encourage describers to include (F1 =59.2; Appendix E.2). This produces scalable, silver-standard\n461\tfull-movie scene segmentation consistent across the AD versions. We combine these with\n462\tscene boundaries derived from the start and end of each video excerpt (Appendix E.2).\n463\tScreenplay Alignment. We align screenplay scenes to movie scenes by representing dialogue and AD jointly as a screenplay-like sequence. We then apply Vecalign (Thompson &\n464\tKoehn, 2019) to perform embedding-based dynamic programming alignment with support\n465\tfor scene insertions, deletions, and many-to-one merges (F1 =80.9 vs. a text-based dialogueonly baseline F1 =63.8; Appendix E.1). We apply this method to all movies with parsed\n466\tscreenplays available in MovieSum (85/206 movies; Saxena & Keller, 2024). This results\n467\tin screenplay segments for 611 videos, after a filtering step that requires that every scene\n468\twithin a video successfully maps to the screenplay.\n469\t\n470\t4\n471\t\n472\tEvaluation Metrics\n473\t\n474\tThe constrained nature of AD lends itself well to evaluation, unlike many other long-form\n475\tgeneration tasks. In particular, AD is anchored to dialogue gaps, description elements\n476\tare typically inserted within a short temporal window of their occurrence (Figure 3), and\n477\tmultiple references are available in our data.\n478\tWe evaluate AD generation under our task formulation, where models must jointly decide\n479\twhat visual information is narratively worth describing, when it can be inserted without\n480\tinterrupting dialogue, and how to express it concisely enough to fit the available time. These\n481\tfactors make no single evaluation metric sufficient. We therefore combine lexical similarity\n482\tmetrics, temporal alignment metrics, and QA-based semantic metrics. CIDEr and METEOR\n483\tprovide interpretable reference-based measures of content similarity. SODA-T evaluates\n484\twhether generated descriptions are temporally aligned with professional AD references,\n485\tusing METEOR-based alignment, while QEval-T evaluates whether generated AD supports\n486\tthe same temporally grounded question answers as the reference. For evaluation on our\n487\tchallenge set, we additionally provide human-level results based on a third expert-created\n488\tAD transcript.\n489\t6\n490\t\n491\t\fPublished as a conference paper at COLM 2026\n492\t\n493\tDialogue Gap-based Evaluation. Our first approach evaluates performance at the level\n494\tof dialogue gaps, directly reflecting the main constraint that applies to AD placement. We\n495\tcollect gaps in the professional dialogue subtitles of at least 1 second, and assign references\n496\tand generations to the gaps that fully contain them (allowing a 1 second gap collar). This\n497\tassignment accounts for 90.2% of reference AD description elements in our challenge set:\n498\t87.8% (N=10,644) for American AD and 93.1% (N=8,734) for British AD, consistent with\n499\tstricter British guidelines regarding AD placement. The remaining cases concern subtitled\n500\taudio that evidently was not considered to preclude AD narration, such as musical lyrics or\n501\tbackground dialogue. These remain covered by our non-dialogue gap metrics. Dialogue\n502\tgaps that are not assigned any reference AD are discarded. Generated text within each gap\n503\tis compared against the corresponding reference texts.\n504\tFor the comparison, we compute CIDEr and METEOR scores. CIDEr natively supports\n505\tmultiple references. However, its length penalty makes it poorly suited to long references.\n506\tFor CIDEr, we therefore only retain dialogue gaps shorter than 20 seconds (see Appendix\n507\tTable 18 for results on longer dialogue gaps). For METEOR, we follow prior work and take\n508\tthe maximum score across references. We retain all dialogue gaps for METEOR.\n509\tAlignment-based Evaluation. Because multiple nearby dialogue gaps may plausibly host\n510\tsome descriptions, strict gap-level matching is too rigid. SODA metrics (Section 2) allow us\n511\tto relax the requirement that generated descriptions must align exactly with the reference\n512\tgap. However, these metrics are unsuitable for direct application to AD, because they rely on\n513\tthe intersection of time spans to align generated and reference descriptions. Our adaptation\n514\tof SODA is operationally similar to the original procedure: we compute an optimal one-toone monotonic alignment between the reference and generated description elements using\n515\tdynamic programming. The key difference in our approach is the alignment criterion. Our\n516\talignment optimizes only semantic similarity (METEOR F1 ) between matched pairs. Sorted\n517\tby start time, let us denote the sequence of n reference description elements as D = (di )in=1\n518\tand the sequence of n̂ generated description elements as D̂ = (dˆj )n̂j=1 . The alignment obtains\n519\tA⋆ = arg maxA ∑\n520\tMETEOR(di , dˆj ), computed exactly with dynamic programming\n521\t(i,j)∈A\n522\t\n523\t(see Appendix L.1). We define SODA-M as the resulting F1 measure over METEOR scores\n524\tand SODA-T as the proportion of matched pairs with timespan midpoints differing by less\n525\tthan τ = 10 seconds (chosen based on Figure 3; Appendix Figure 10 shows a sweep):\n526\t\n527\tSODA-M =\n528\t\n529\t∑(i,j)∈A⋆ METEOR(di , dˆj )\n530\t,\n531\t\u0001\n532\t1\n533\t2 |D| + | D̂|\n534\t\n535\tSODA-T =\n536\t\n537\t\u0003\n538\t\u0002\n539\t1\n540\t1 |m( Ii ) − m( Îj )| < τ\n541\t∑\n542\t|D| (i,j)∈A⋆\n543\t\n544\twhere m( Ii ) is the midpoint of the time interval Ii of description element di . Following prior\n545\tSODA implementations, we take the maximum of each score across references.\n546\tQA-based Evaluation. We assess whether generated AD supports downstream understanding using a QA-based evaluation that captures both semantic correctness and temporal\n547\tgrounding. We prompt OLMo 3 32B Think to generate multiple-choice questions from\n548\treference AD. Then, we filter aggressively for quality, retaining only questions answered\n549\tcorrectly by OLMo 3 32B Base using each reference AD version and incorrectly when the\n550\tsupporting AD segment is removed. This ensures that questions depend on specific narrative content described in both AD references and that the wrong answer distractors are\n551\tappropriate. Questions are answered using the base LLM by comparing multiple-choice\n552\tlogits. Prompts and more details on filtering are in Appendix L.2.\n553\tWe define QEval and QEval-T, capturing semantic accuracy and temporally grounded\n554\taccuracy. For each question, let y j and ŷ j denote the gold-standard and predicted answers.\n555\tIj is given by the segment used to generate the question. We predict Îj as the segment\n556\twhose removal most reduces the probability of the predicted answer. This attribution-based\n557\tapproach identifies which part of the generated AD supports the answer, and achieves\n558\tround-trip consistency of 92.6% (see stages 3→4 in Appendix Table 13).\n559\t7\n560\t\n561\t\fPublished as a conference paper at COLM 2026\n562\t\n563\tQEval measures answer correctness (the proportion of questions with system-predicted\n564\tanswer matching the gold-standard answer) and QEval-T also enforces temporal alignment:\n565\tQEval =\n566\t\n567\t1 N\n568\t1 N\n569\t1(ŷ j = y j ) ; QEval-T =\n570\t1(ŷ j = y j ) · C j ; C j = 1(|m( Îj ) − m( Ij )| < τ )\n571\t∑\n572\tN j =1\n573\tN j∑\n574\t=1\n575\t\n576\twhere τ = 10s (Figure 3). QEval-T therefore passes judgement on both the inclusion\n577\tof specific narrative content (correct answer) and when it is grounded (correct temporal\n578\talignment). We retain only QA pairs that satisfy QEval-T under both reference AD versions;\n579\ton average, there is one QA pair for every 3.6 reference description elements. Significance\n580\ttesting uses two-tailed Monte Carlo permutation tests with R = 10,000 and α = 0.05.\n581\t\n582\t5\n583\t\n584\tExperiments\n585\t\n586\t5.1\n587\t\n588\tExperimental Setup\n589\t\n590\tWe demonstrate the application of our evaluation metrics to the Re frame d challenge set;\n591\tresults on the test set are in Appendix N. Our objectives are to assess: (1) upper and lower\n592\tbounds on task performance; (2) progress made by pre-existing models; and (3) the extent\n593\tto which current LLMs can perform our task.\n594\tUpper and Lower Bounds. As an approximate upper bound, we take a third professional\n595\tAD transcript (available for one challenge movie) and evaluate it as a system output against\n596\tthe remaining two references. This gives a human-expert ceiling: i.e., the score a professional\n597\tdescriber achieves on our metrics when judged against other professionals describing the\n598\tsame film. As a lower bound, we show results from a greedy baseline that fills each dialogue\n599\tgap with randomly sampled training-set description elements until the gap is full and no\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"28a31d0a-d593-4419-87c8-544579cdfc2c","uuid":"7e312661-bb09-4ae9-843d-55bef7b23fd5","timestamp":"2026-08-11T12:03:19.523Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/reframed.txt","content":"and SDH subtitles, which include salient non-verbal audio for hard-of-hearing audiences.\nMoreover, we adapt existing evaluation metrics to our novel AD task and benchmark\ncurrent AD generation systems and LLMs under this formulation. Our contributions are:\n• A realistic task formulation for AD generation (a model must decide when and\nwhat to describe), together with evaluation metrics that exploit multiple references.\n• Re frame d, a dataset of 2,023 video excerpts (3,302 scenes) from 206 movies with\ndual AD versions (US and UK), dialogue subtitles, SDH, screenplays, and video.\n• The Re frame d challenge set of 10 fully manually annotated movies with 2–3\nprofessionally transcribed AD versions each, scene boundaries, AD-to-scene labels,\nscreenplay alignment, and professional subtitles.\n• A suite of benchmarks for ASR quality, temporal alignment, screenplay alignment,\nscene segmentation, AD splitting, and AD generation on the above data.\n\n2\n\n\fPublished as a conference paper at COLM 2026\n\nSCENE 1\n\nSCENE 25\n\n...\n\nSCENE 50\n\n...\n\nSCENE 75\n\n...\n\nSCENE 100\n\n...\n\nSCENE 120\n\nAligned\nscreenplay\n\n...\n\nVideo\nexcerpts\n\n...\n\n...\n\n...\n\n[MIKAEL]\nWe’re late.\n\n?\n\n[ERIKA]\nFashionably\nlate.\n\n?\n\n?\n\n...\n\nDialogue\nsubtitles\n\nShe watches numbly as the couple walk\naway with their arms around each other.\n\n...\n\nAD\n\nAD Generation\nShe takes a package from the\n\n. . . back of the bike, but stops dead\nwhen she sees Mikael leave his\napartment with Erika.\n\nDialogue\n\nDialogue gap\n\nAD\n\nNo AD\n\nFigure 2: The Re frame d task formulation. Given input of a video, dialogue subtitles,\nand the spans of gaps between them, a model must decide both when and what to describe.\nNarrative context can be made available from the movie’s screenplay and other scene videos.\n\n2\n\nRelated Work\n\nAD as Video Captioning. The task has commonly been framed as a form of video captioning:\ngiven a short video clip, generate a natural language sentence that describes its visually\nsalient content. Most current work follows this formulation and finetunes multimodal\nmodels for the task, augmenting input with additional signals such as broader visual\ncontext, character information, or scene-level cues (Wang et al., 2021; Han et al., 2023a;b;\n2024; Lin et al., 2024; Lee et al., 2025; Deganutti et al., 2025; Wang et al., 2025; Ye et al.,\n2025). DistinctAD (Fang et al., 2025) trains using character information, visual features and\ntext from nearby clips, with architectural components and learning objectives that reward\ndistinctive descriptions. Xie et al. (2024) propose to first generate a detailed description\nof a clip and then summarize it into AD; in Shot-by-Shot (Xie et al., 2025), this approach\nis improved by incorporating knowledge of shot boundaries and scale. Other work relies\non LLM prompting (Lin et al., 2023; Chu et al., 2024; Zhang et al., 2024; Ye et al., 2024;\nKhandelwal et al., 2025); Gao et al. (2025) provide a broader survey of the area. A related\nline of work incorporates screenplays as narrative context. Screenplays have been estimated\nto contain approximately 60% of the information required for AD generation (Lakritz &\nSalway, 2006), and later work shows that screenplay context can improve generation quality\n(Rimle et al., 2020; Campos et al., 2023; Park et al., 2025). Despite this progress, all these\napproaches address a simplified formulation of AD creation: they assume AD placement\nis given and only generate the description sentence. Our benchmark is the first to require\nsystems to determine placement and content jointly.\nMovie Datasets with AD. Many movie datasets exist without AD (Tapaswi et al., 2016;\nGu et al., 2018; Vicol et al., 2018; Huang et al., 2020; Bain et al., 2020; Wu & Krahenbuhl,\n2021; Sun et al., 2025; 2024; Wu et al., 2024; Zaranis et al., 2025). The first large-scale\ndataset containing AD is LSMDC (Rohrbach et al., 2017), which pairs semi-automatically\ntranscribed AD sentences with manually extracted movie clips that correspond with the\ndescribed elements, replacing character names with placeholders. MAD (Soldan et al., 2022)\nscales this up by fully automatically transcribing AD tracks and aligning them to full movies\n3\n\n\fPublished as a conference paper at COLM 2026\n\nvia audio cross-correlation. Evaluation material reuses LSMDC timecoding, which is suited\nto their task of video grounding. Han et al. (2023a) repurpose a subset of this evaluation\ndata for a full-movie AD generation benchmark; the adoption of the LSMDC timecoding\nmeans that the time-span of the element to be described in the generation is provided as\ninput. The placeholder substitution for character names is also retained. Han et al. (2024)\nintroduce CMD-AD by aligning AD segments to movie scenes from YouTube and allowing\ntemporal offsets and speed adjustments to account for differences in broadcast frame rates\n(e.g., 23.976fps NTSC vs. 25fps PAL).\nThese datasets share three limitations. First, AD transcripts are produced automatically, yet\ntranscription quality has never been evaluated; in Appendix C.1 we show this is a major\nissue. Second, the alignment of AD tracks to movie scenes has also not been validated; in\nAppendix C.2 we show that the method used for the construction of CMD-AD is very noisy.\nThird, the MAD-based evaluation set inherits LSMDC timecoding, transcription errors and\ncharacter name replacement, issues that have propagated into subsequent work.\nEvaluation of AD Generation. Appendix Table 3 summarizes the many metrics used to\nevaluate AD generation. They include n-gram (e.g. METEOR, Banerjee & Lavie, 2005),\nimage-captioning (e.g. CIDEr, Vedantam et al., 2015), and QA-based evaluation (Kala et al.,\n2025). Dense video captioning metrics such as SODA (Fujita et al., 2020) and its temporal\ncounterpart (Batra et al., 2022) align generated and reference elements using their time\nspans before scoring them with F-measure. However, these metrics inherit limitations from\nthe simplified task setting. Because prior work assumes fixed AD placement, evaluation\noperates at the level of individual captions matched to predefined clips and therefore cannot\nassess whether a system correctly decides when to insert a description as well as what visual\nelement needs to be described. Prior work also ignores the task’s subjectivity and typically\nuses only a single reference. We adapt existing metrics to our open-ended task formulation,\nand use multiple references throughout.\n\n3\n\nThe Re frame d Dataset\n\nWe now provide an overview of the Re frame d dataset and its construction, including\nquantitative validation of each tool used (results are summarized in Section 3.2, with more\ndetails in Appendix C and E). Our design is guided by the goal of supporting AD generation\nas a joint decision problem over when and what to describe, informed by narrative context.\n3.1\n\nData and Benchmark Overview\n\nThe dataset is built from movie excerpts licensed by Fandango, with raw videos without\nwatermarking available publicly on rottentomatoes.com. Narrative context is provided\nfrom full-movie screenplays that we align to the movie timelines as well as other videos\nfrom the same movie. We select movies with at least five movie excerpt videos and listings\non audiovault.net for both American and British AD versions. Table 1 compares our\ndataset against publicly available alternatives, showing our contributions of providing more\nparallel data streams for models to learn from, multiple AD references for both training and\nevaluation material, and gold standard data for evaluation. Following prior work (Bamman\net al., 2024), we exclude animated movies as they represent a substantially different visual\ndomain.\nAD Transcription and Alignment. We obtain transcription for all AD tracks: for evaluation\nmovies, they are the result of professional human transcription; training movies rely on a\nSpeechmatics-based pipeline (Speechmatics are a specialist ASR and diarization provider).\nFor each movie, we align the video excerpts to the full AD track (both American and British\nversions) using cross-correlation, accounting for possible PAL–NTSC speed differences and\nenforcing consistent alignment offsets across scenes from the same movie.\nParallel Data Streams. For each movie excerpt, we provide professional English dialogueonly and SDH subtitles sourced from opensubtitles.org. They are aligned to video using\nASR-based timestamp matching (Appendix G). For the 85 movies with parsed screenplays\navailable in MovieSum (Saxena & Keller, 2024), we align screenplay scenes to movie scenes\n4\n\n\fPublished as a conference paper at COLM 2026\n\nTRAINING\nhrs #refs\n\nTESTING\nTASK\nhrs #refs\n\nName\n\nType\n\nLSMDC (AD)\nMAD\nMAD-v2-eval\nMAD-v3-eval\nRe frame d\n\n5s clips\nfull movies\nfull movies\nfull movies\nfull movies\n\n✔ ✔ ✔ ✘\n✘ ✘ ✘ ✘\n– – – –\n– – – –\n– – – –\n\n✔ 134\n✔ 990\n–\n–\n–\n–\n–\n–\n\n1\n1\n–\n–\n–\n\n✔ ✔ ✔ ✘ ✔ 12\n✘ ✘ ✘ ✘ ✔ 217\n✘ ✘ ✔ ✘ ✔ 19\n✘ ✘ ✔ ✘ ✔ 19\n✔ ✔ ✔ ✔ ✔ 21\n\n1\n1\n1\n1\n2-3\n\nVC\nVG\nVC\nASR\nAD\n\nCMD-AD\nRe frame d\n\n2m scenes\n2m scenes\n\n✔ ✔ ✔ ✘ ✔ 307\n✔ ✔ ✔ ✔ ✔ 77\n\n1\n2\n\n✔ ✔ ✔ ✘ ✔\n✔ ✔ ✔ ✔ ✔\n\n22\n4\n\n1\n2\n\nVC\nAD\n\nTable 1: Comparison of publicly available AD datasets. We report whether video ( ),\naudio ( ), subtitles ( ), screenplays ( ), and AD transcripts ( ) are available at goldstandard quality (✔), silver-standard quality (✔; transcription or alignment), or not at all\n(✘), alongside total hours of content and number of AD references. VC = video captioning;\nVG = video grounding; ASR = automatic speech recognition (and diarization) of AD; AD =\nour task of AD generation. Training includes validation data, if it is provided. CMD-AD\nhours are based on the available video data. Our contributions are highlighted.\nusing Vecalign (Thompson & Koehn, 2019) applied to joint dialogue and AD representations,\nenabling robust alignment despite script deviations.\nAD Splitting and Scene Segmentation. We split AD transcripts into segments that each\ncorrespond to one visual element. For all evaluation data, the splitting is a part of the\nprofessional human transcription; for training data, it is from a learned splitting model.\nWe additionally apply a second learned model that predicts scene boundaries from AD\nsegments. Appendix K shows split elements for our earlier example.\nDataset Splits. The final dataset contains 206 movies and 2,023 video excerpts (average\nduration 144 seconds), spanning 3,302 scenes; 85 movies have aligned screenplays. We\nsplit the data into training, validation, and test sets such that the genre distribution is\nbalanced across the splits (Appendix I). The training set excludes movies appearing in\nprevious evaluation sets. The validation set consists of 10 movies from existing evaluation\ndatasets, with aligned screenplays, while the test set contains 10 recent movies (2023–2025)\nnot present in prior datasets. Validation and test movie excerpts benefit from professionally\ntranscribed AD rather than automatic transcription (Appendix B).\nChallenge Benchmark. For full-movie evaluation, we provide a manually annotated\nbenchmark of 10 movies, each with 2–3 AD versions, exact scene boundaries, per-element\nscene labels, scene sequences, screenplay alignment, and professional dialogue and SDH\nsubtitles (Appendix D). All annotations were performed manually, either by a professional\npost-production company or by a trained annotator. Seven of these movies are drawn from\nMAD-v3-eval; we additionally include Dune: Part Two (2024), Barbie (2023), and Priscilla\n(2023), selected for their award-winning AD tracks. The resulting set of ten movies spans\n20 years of cinema and covers a diverse range of genres — 14 out of the 19 IMDb genre\ncategories in the training set are represented.\n3.2\n\nThe Construction Pipeline\n\nGold-standard Transcription. All downstream evaluation depends on gold-standard AD\ntranscription, which is unavailable from existing data. We hence contracted a film postproduction company to perform fully manual AD transcription and timecoding to the\nnearest frame (approximately 0.04 seconds), and to provide segments each corresponding\nto a single visual element of the movie, which we will call description elements. We call this\nnew resource MAD-v3-eval. Analysis of these annotations shows that AD narration occurs\nwithin 10 seconds of the described elements, but not necessarily at exactly the same time as\nthem, reflecting the need to place AD within available dialogue gaps (Figure 3).\nAD Transcription and Diarization. The first pipeline stage takes a full-length AD audio\ntrack as input and produces a transcription with speaker labels and timestamps. We\n5\n\n\fPublished as a conference paper at COLM 2026\n\nFigure 3: Histogram comparing (a) MADv2-eval timecoding (v2; when described\nelements occur) and (b) new professional\ntimecoding (v3; when the AD is spoken).\nTexts are normalized and aligned at the\nword level, and v2 segments matching\na concatenation of one or more v3 segments are retained. We plot the midpoint\ndifferences.\n\n300\n\n−10\n\n−5\n\n0\n\n5\n\n10\n\n∆ AD spoken vs. event occurred (secs)\n\nevaluate multiple ASR systems against MAD-v3-eval and find that existing AD corpora are\nextremely noisy (WhisperX achieves a best overall word error rate, WER=12.8; the MADv2-eval transcriptions achieve 22.1). A system based on Speechmatics with character-name\nadaptation achieves WER=2.9 and is used to transcribe all training data (Appendix C.1).\nTemporal Alignment. The second stage aligns scene videos to full-length AD tracks using\ncross-correlation (Soldan et al., 2022), accounting for frame-rate differences between PAL\nand NTSC formats (Appendix Figure 5). This approach is robust for typical scene durations\n(100% alignment accuracy to within one frame for scenes of length 32 seconds or greater,\nwhich accounts for >99% of the video extracts in our data), and we enforce consistency of\nalignment offsets across scenes within each movie (Appendix C.2).\nAD Splitting and Scene Segmentation. ASR produces sentence-level output, but our\nevaluation data is segmented into description elements. We therefore segment transcribed\nAD sentences using a RoBERTa-based model trained on gold-standard annotations (F1 =64.8\nvs. a comma-splitting baseline F1 =36.9; Appendix E.3). A classifier over these segments then\nidentifies scene boundaries, exploiting textual cues (e.g., “Now”) that AD guidelines encourage describers to include (F1 =59.2; Appendix E.2). This produces scalable, silver-standard\nfull-movie scene segmentation consistent across the AD versions. We combine these with\nscene boundaries derived from the start and end of each video excerpt (Appendix E.2).\nScreenplay Alignment. We align screenplay scenes to movie scenes by representing dialogue and AD jointly as a screenplay-like sequence. We then apply Vecalign (Thompson &\nKoehn, 2019) to perform embedding-based dynamic programming alignment with support\nfor scene insertions, deletions, and many-to-one merges (F1 =80.9 vs. a text-based dialogueonly baseline F1 =63.8; Appendix E.1). We apply this method to all movies with parsed\nscreenplays available in MovieSum (85/206 movies; Saxena & Keller, 2024). This results\nin screenplay segments for 611 videos, after a filtering step that requires that every scene\nwithin a video successfully maps to the screenplay.\n\n4\n\nEvaluation Metrics\n\nThe constrained nature of AD lends itself well to evaluation, unlike many other long-form\ngeneration tasks. In particular, AD is anchored to dialogue gaps, description elements\nare typically inserted within a short temporal window of their occurrence (Figure 3), and\nmultiple references are available in our data.\nWe evaluate AD generation under our task formulation, where models must jointly decide\nwhat visual information is narratively worth describing, when it can be inserted without\ninterrupting dialogue, and how to express it concisely enough to fit the available time. These\nfactors make no single evaluation metric sufficient. We therefore combine lexical similarity\nmetrics, temporal alignment metrics, and QA-based semantic metrics. CIDEr and METEOR\nprovide interpretable reference-based measures of content similarity. SODA-T evaluates\nwhether generated descriptions are temporally aligned with professional AD references,\nusing METEOR-based alignment, while QEval-T evaluates whether generated AD supports\nthe same temporally grounded question answers as the reference. For evaluation on our\nchallenge set, we additionally provide human-level results based on a third expert-created\nAD transcript.\n6\n\n\fPublished as a conference paper at COLM 2026\n\nDialogue Gap-based Evaluation. Our first approach evaluates performance at the level\nof dialogue gaps, directly reflecting the main constraint that applies to AD placement. We\ncollect gaps in the professional dialogue subtitles of at least 1 second, and assign references\nand generations to the gaps that fully contain them (allowing a 1 second gap collar). This\nassignment accounts for 90.2% of reference AD description elements in our challenge set:\n87.8% (N=10,644) for American AD and 93.1% (N=8,734) for British AD, consistent with\nstricter British guidelines regarding AD placement. The remaining cases concern subtitled\naudio that evidently was not considered to preclude AD narration, such as musical lyrics or\nbackground dialogue. These remain covered by our non-dialogue gap metrics. Dialogue\ngaps that are not assigned any reference AD are discarded. Generated text within each gap\nis compared against the corresponding reference texts.\nFor the comparison, we compute CIDEr and METEOR scores. CIDEr natively supports\nmultiple references. However, its length penalty makes it poorly suited to long references.\nFor CIDEr, we therefore only retain dialogue gaps shorter than 20 seconds (see Appendix\nTable 18 for results on longer dialogue gaps). For METEOR, we follow prior work and take\nthe maximum score across references. We retain all dialogue gaps for METEOR.\nAlignment-based Evaluation. Because multiple nearby dialogue gaps may plausibly host\nsome descriptions, strict gap-level matching is too rigid. SODA metrics (Section 2) allow us\nto relax the requirement that generated descriptions must align exactly with the reference\ngap. However, these metrics are unsuitable for direct application to AD, because they rely on\nthe intersection of time spans to align generated and reference descriptions. Our adaptation\nof SODA is operationally similar to the original procedure: we compute an optimal one-toone monotonic alignment between the reference and generated description elements using\ndynamic programming. The key difference in our approach is the alignment criterion. Our\nalignment optimizes only semantic similarity (METEOR F1 ) between matched pairs. Sorted\nby start time, let us denote the sequence of n reference description elements as D = (di )in=1\nand the sequence of n̂ generated description elements as D̂ = (dˆj )n̂j=1 . The alignment obtains\nA⋆ = arg maxA ∑\nMETEOR(di , dˆj ), computed exactly with dynamic programming\n(i,j)∈A\n\n(see Appendix L.1). We define SODA-M as the resulting F1 measure over METEOR scores\nand SODA-T as the proportion of matched pairs with timespan midpoints differing by less\nthan τ = 10 seconds (chosen based on Figure 3; Appendix Figure 10 shows a sweep):\n\nSODA-M =\n\n∑(i,j)∈A⋆ METEOR(di , dˆj )\n,\n\u0001\n1\n2 |D| + | D̂|\n\nSODA-T =\n\n\u0003\n\u0002\n1\n1 |m( Ii ) − m( Îj )| < τ\n∑\n|D| (i,j)∈A⋆\n\nwhere m( Ii ) is the midpoint of the time interval Ii of description element di . Following prior\nSODA implementations, we take the maximum of each score across references.\nQA-based Evaluation. We assess whether generated AD supports downstream understanding using a QA-based evaluation that captures both semantic correctness and temporal\ngrounding. We prompt OLMo 3 32B Think to generate multiple-choice questions from\nreference AD. Then, we filter aggressively for quality, retaining only questions answered\ncorrectly by OLMo 3 32B Base using each reference AD version and incorrectly when the\nsupporting AD segment is removed. This ensures that questions depend on specific narrative content described in both AD references and that the wrong answer distractors are\nappropriate. Questions are answered using the base LLM by comparing multiple-choice\nlogits. Prompts and more details on filtering are in Appendix L.2.\nWe define QEval and QEval-T, capturing semantic accuracy and temporally grounded\naccuracy. For each question, let y j and ŷ j denote the gold-standard and predicted answers.\nIj is given by the segment used to generate the question. We predict Îj as the segment\nwhose removal most reduces the probability of the predicted answer. This attribution-based\napproach identifies which part of the generated AD supports the answer, and achieves\nround-trip consistency of 92.6% (see stages 3→4 in Appendix Table 13).\n7\n\n\fPublished as a conference paper at COLM 2026\n\nQEval measures answer correctness (the proportion of questions with system-predicted\nanswer matching the gold-standard answer) and QEval-T also enforces temporal alignment:\nQEval =\n\n1 N\n1 N\n1(ŷ j = y j ) ; QEval-T =\n1(ŷ j = y j ) · C j ; C j = 1(|m( Îj ) − m( Ij )| < τ )\n∑\nN j =1\nN j∑\n=1\n\nwhere τ = 10s (Figure 3). QEval-T therefore passes judgement on both the inclusion\nof specific narrative content (correct answer) and when it is grounded (correct temporal\nalignment). We retain only QA pairs that satisfy QEval-T under both reference AD versions;\non average, there is one QA pair for every 3.6 reference description elements. Significance\ntesting uses two-tailed Monte Carlo permutation tests with R = 10,000 and α = 0.05.\n\n5\n\nExperiments\n\n5.1\n\nExperimental Setup\n\nWe demonstrate the application of our evaluation metrics to the Re frame d challenge set;\nresults on the test set are in Appendix N. Our objectives are to assess: (1) upper and lower\nbounds on task performance; (2) progress made by pre-existing models; and (3) the extent\nto which current LLMs can perform our task.\nUpper and Lower Bounds. As an approximate upper bound, we take a third professional\nAD transcript (available for one challenge movie) and evaluate it as a system output against\nthe remaining two references. This gives a human-expert ceiling: i.e., the score a professional\ndescriber achieves on our metrics when judged against other professionals describing the\nsame film. As a lower bound, we show results from a greedy baseline that fills each dialogue\ngap with randomly sampled training-set description elements until the gap is full and no","numLines":500,"startLine":100,"totalLines":3998}}}
