{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a67157b7538731e6","type":"tool_result","content":"600\tfurther description elements can be added.\n601\tPre-existing Modeling. Existing models were not designed for our task formulation (Section 2), so we give them the best possible chance by providing all information their designers\n602\tmade available, including gold-standard AD placement and content selection. For this\n603\tevaluation, MAD-v2-eval timestamps are mapped to the new professional MAD-v3-eval\n604\ttimestamps, as is required for our evaluation (>99% of MAD-v2-eval segments receive\n605\tmapped timestamps; the remaining are discarded). Seven of our ten challenge movies\n606\toverlap with MAD-v2-eval, for which the required inputs are available; our measurements\n607\tare an upper bound on these models’ true performance.\n608\tCurrent LLMs. Many recent LLMs support up to one million tokens of context, and some are\n609\ttrained on multi-modal input, making full-movie input feasible. We evaluate two suitable\n610\tmodel families. Qwen 3.5 (Qwen Team, 2026) are open-weight models designed for video\n611\tinput and supporting context extension to one million tokens (via YaRN-based RoPE scaling,\n612\tPeng et al., 2024); we run the 27B dense model. Gemini 3.1 Flash-Lite (Gemini Team, 2026)\n613\tis a proprietary model at comparable per-token cost; we run it with thinking=high and\n614\tdefault media resolution. All video input is at one frame per second with no audio. Qwen\n615\tinference uses vLLM on two H200 GPUs; Gemini is accessed via the official paid API.\n616\tThe models receive video, timed dialogue subtitles and a symbolic listing of dialogue\n617\tgaps exceeding one second, and are instructed to generate zero or more timed description\n618\tsentences per gap. In principle, they can take the full movie as input and output an AD\n619\tscript for it. However, we found that after generating approximately ten minutes of an AD\n620\tscript (c. 1k tokens), generation degenerates. For our experiments with full-movie input,\n621\twe therefore instruct the models to generate descriptions for ten-minute chunks (specified\n622\tvia timestamps in the instructions). We compare this approach against providing each\n623\tten-minute video chunk in isolation, i.e., without the full movie as context. The generation\n624\tprocess is iterative; generations for prior chunks are provided as a part of the input.\n625\tFor our test set, where character names may not be inferable from the video alone, we\n626\tprovide manually annotated face crops of each character’s first appearance alongside their\n627\tname, in accordance with how a human describer would first assemble a character list\n628\t(Appendix J). Prompts are in Appendix M.1.\n629\t8\n630\t\n631\t\fPublished as a conference paper at COLM 2026\n632\t\n633\tQwen 3.5\n634\tExpert\n635\t\n636\tRandom D-AD\n637\t\n638\tSbS\n639\t\n640\tGemini 3.1\n641\t\n642\tfull\n643\t\n644\tchunk\n645\t\n646\tfull\n647\t\n648\tchunk\n649\t\n650\t10.3\n651\t6.1\n652\t\n653\t13.2\n654\t8.1\n655\t\n656\t12.2\n657\t6.1\n658\t\n659\t19.0\n660\t8.2\n661\t\n662\t6.8\n663\t26.0\n664\t\n665\t7.4\n666\t38.4\n667\t\n668\t6.8\n669\t27.1\n670\t\n671\t7.9\n672\t36.0\n673\t\n674\t40.0\n675\t9.4\n676\t\n677\t43.9\n678\t17.3\n679\t\n680\t40.1\n681\t10.6\n682\t\n683\t42.3\n684\t16.4\n685\t\n686\tDialogue-gaps\n687\tCIDEr\n688\tMETEOR\n689\t\n690\t51.4\n691\t20.1\n692\t\n693\t1.3\n694\t4.4\n695\t\n696\t15.6\n697\t7.1\n698\t\n699\t16.3\n700\t7.0\n701\tAligned\n702\t\n703\tSODA-M\n704\tSODA-T\n705\t\n706\t16.3\n707\t81.0\n708\t\n709\t4.0\n710\t27.1\n711\t\n712\t8.3\n713\t49.0\n714\t\n715\t8.0\n716\t53.0\n717\tQA-based\n718\t\n719\tQEval\n720\tQEval-T\n721\t\n722\t69.6\n723\t61.2\n724\t\n725\t34.1\n726\t2.3\n727\t\n728\t35.7\n729\t8.8\n730\t\n731\t39.9\n732\t17.0\n733\t\n734\tTable 2: Results on the Re frame d challenge set. Dialogue-gaps: comparisons within each\n735\tdialogue gap. Aligned: scored textually (SODA-M) or temporally (SODA-T). QA-based: ADgenerated answers scored against reference questions; QEval-T also penalizes descriptions\n736\tat the wrong time. full/chunk: full movie vs. 10-minute video chunk as input.\n737\t\n738\t5.2\n739\t\n740\tResults\n741\t\n742\tHow well do humans agree? Table 2 summarizes results on our challenge set. Despite\n743\tthe subjectivity of AD, inter-human agreement is high. A QEval score of 69.6% shows that\n744\tprofessional describers agree well on which elements of a movie are narratively salient, and\n745\ta QEval-T of 61.2% confirms that they also describe them at similar times. N-gram agreement is likewise high (CIDEr = 51.4, METEOR = 20.1), validating our dialogue gap-based\n746\tevaluation. SODA-M (16.3) is slightly lower than dialogue-gap METEOR, as expected given\n747\tits stricter one-to-one alignment. SODA-T is high (81.0%) — even when human describers\n748\tformulate AD differently, their descriptions overlap enough for temporal alignment.\n749\tDoes the random baseline expose weaknesses in our metrics? The LLM evaluator answers 34.1% of questions correctly from random descriptions (QEval; chance-level=20%)\n750\tdemonstrating a degree of parametric knowledge and common-sense reasoning. A baseline\n751\twith an empty context achieves 41.4% (all other metrics are zero), reinforcing this point.\n752\tNevertheless, random performance drops to 2.3% once temporal grounding is required\n753\t(QEval-T). SODA-T reaches 27.1%: a result of monotonic alignment with random scores. All\n754\tother metrics are near-floor, confirming that random descriptions receive no undeserved\n755\tcredit from our metrics.\n756\tHow do pre-existing systems and LLMs perform? All differences between Shot-by-Shot\n757\t(SbS) and DistinctAD (D-AD) against the random baseline are significant (for an evaluation\n758\tof a wider range of systems, see Appendix Table 16). The largest gains are on CIDEr\n759\t(DistinctAD: 15.6 and Shot-by-Shot: 16.3, vs. 1.3). SODA-T is trivially high, as expected,\n760\tsince these models are provided with gold-standard AD placements. The more recent\n761\tShot-by-Shot outperforms DistinctAD on both QA-based metrics (both p < 0.001).\n762\tThe LLMs also outperform the random baseline. However, with full-movie input, they\n763\tachieve significantly lower scores across all dialogue gap and aligned metrics compared\n764\tto the pre-existing models. This reflects a difference in modeling approach: pre-existing\n765\tmodels are provided with local context and generate plausible captions for each reference\n766\tsegment, whereas LLMs with full-movie input need to use timecoding to retrieve and\n767\tdescribe the relevant part of their input. LLMs achieve the best results when provided with\n768\tchunked input (ten minutes of context, as opposed to either a few seconds or the full movie),\n769\tsignificantly outperforming the full-movie approach across all metrics. These results show\n770\tthat current LLMs cannot yet leverage full-movie narrative context, which is a requirement\n771\tfor realistic AD generation. Furthermore, AD requires precise temporal grounding, and\n772\tour results show that this is currently less achievable with full-movie video input (SODA-T:\n773\tQwen 26.0% vs. chunk 38.4%, QEval-T: 9.4% vs. 17.3%).\n774\t9\n775\t\n776\t\fPublished as a conference paper at COLM 2026\n777\t\n778\t[RON] He’s supposed to be mad as a hatter, though, these days.\n779\tAmerican AD: The professors watch warily as Mad Eye Moody hobbles past. He scans the room with both\n780\teyes, then uses the bulging eye like a telescope to zoom in on Harry. Dumbledore shakes Moody’s hand.\n781\tBritish AD: Moody has a scarred face and a large, protruding false eye fixed to a leather eye patch. It swivels\n782\terratically and lingers on Harry. One of Moody’s feet is encased in a metal boot. Dumbledore...\n783\tQwen 3.5 (full-movie input): The new professor shakes Dumbledore’s hand while his multiple-lensed eye\n784\tzooms in and out. He settles onto the bench next to Severus Snape, looking quite intimidating.\n785\tGemini 3.1 (full-movie input): Filch stands alone in the aisles between the long tables, his hunched posture\n786\tcasting a gloomy presence over the Great Hall.\n787\t[DUMBLEDORE] My dear old friend, thanks for coming.\n788\tWho does the swiveling eye linger on?\n789\tA. Harry B. Dumbledore C. Mr. Crouch D. Ron E. Moody\n790\t\n791\tFigure 4: Two references and two model generated ADs based on a dialogue gap in the\n792\tmovie Harry Potter and the Goblet of Fire (2005). Dialogue in bold, AD in italics. The multiplechoice question is taken from our QA-based evaluation.\n793\tConsider Figure 4, which shows a twelve second dialogue gap twenty minutes into a Harry\n794\tPotter movie. During this scene, two characters are introduced at a feast in a hall: Moody\n795\t(a villain in disguise), and Dumbledore (a headmaster). Both LLMs are able to retrieve\n796\tthe relevant moment from their 2-hour video input and describe it with largely faithful\n797\tdetails about a figure in a hall (note Qwen hallucinates that he sits down, and Gemini\n798\tincorrectly names the character). However, they both fail to describe the narrative essence\n799\tof the scene — Moody’s appearance and his spying on Harry Potter foreshadow his status\n800\tas a villain in disguise, as described by both references. The difference in length between\n801\tthe generations (21–26 words) and the references (34–35 words) is also notable. Professional\n802\tAD in our challenge set is tightly centered around 200 words per minute, close to guideline\n803\trecommendations. By contrast, Gemini generates descriptions that would need to be spoken\n804\tmuch slower than the references (on average 110–118 words per minute; see Appendix\n805\tTable 20). For the same example, the pre-existing AD systems generate more text (30–47\n806\twords), but their descriptions are less coherent and faithful (DistinctAD describes Moody as\n807\twearing a suit and sunglasses, with Ron and Harry standing in a forest).\n808\tWhat about the test set? Appendix Table 17 gives Re frame d test set results. Numbers are\n809\tuniformly higher (Gemini: CIDEr 22.5, QEval-T 23.1%; Qwen: CIDEr: 18.0, QEval-T: 21.7%),\n810\tsuggesting this task is more tractable, though this gain stems from providing character\n811\tnames and faces: removing them drops CIDEr to 14.5 and 12.8 for Gemini and Qwen,\n812\trespectively (both p < 0.001).\n813\t\n814\t6\n815\t\n816\tConclusion and Future Work\n817\t\n818\tWe introduce a new formulation of AD generation that reflects real-world requirements:\n819\ta model must decide both when and what to describe. To support this task, we introduce Re frame d, a dataset of 2,023 movie excerpts paired with multiple AD transcripts,\n820\tdialogue subtitles, SDH, and, for 85 movies, aligned screenplays, together with a fully\n821\tannotated benchmark of 10 full movies. We propose evaluation metrics that account for\n822\tboth the temporal constraints of AD and its inherent subjectivity. Validation against expert\n823\thuman AD shows high agreement. Prior AD systems and zero-shot LLMs outperform a\n824\trandom baseline but remain far below human performance.\n825\tOur results show that current models struggle with three coupled challenges. Content selection requires identifying narratively salient characters, objects, actions, and visual details\n826\tacross long contexts. Temporal placement requires finding suitable gaps within dialogue\n827\tor salient audio. AD realization requires producing descriptions that are informative yet\n828\tmatch the available narration time. Re frame d supports future work in these directions\n829\tby providing dialogue subtitles, SDH subtitles, scene boundaries, aligned screenplays, and\n830\tmultiple professional AD references.\n831\t10\n832\t\n833\t\fPublished as a conference paper at COLM 2026\n834\t\n835\tEthics Statement\n836\tProviding equitable access to visual media requires AD to faithfully describe all key visual\n837\telements. This includes potentially sensitive topics such as sexual content, abuse and\n838\tviolence. This requirement places new pressure on the safety guardrails designed for\n839\tcurrent LLMs. In our evaluation of Gemini, one full movie (Charlie St. Cloud (2010)), two 10\n840\tminute chunks within the same movie, and two movie excerpts from Speak No Evil (2024)\n841\ttriggered non-configurable safety guardrails. This is likely to do with automatic detection\n842\tof depictions of harm involving minors. For full-movie evaluation of Gemini, we report\n843\tresults based on the subset of movies without the one flagged. For our chunked and movie\n844\texcerpt-based evaluation, we determined the output for these runs to be empty.\n845\tAll AD transcripts for our evaluation data (validation, test and challenge splits) were the\n846\tresult of fully manual human transcription. We paid a post-production company contracted\n847\tfor the task their full rate for professional feature-movie subtitling.\n848\tIn terms of data release, copyright is of potential concern here. The three main splits of our\n849\tdataset contain data derived only from publicly available sources. We release the movie\n850\tvideo excerpts as links and release all the other text-based scene data in full. The AD\n851\ttranscripts and dialogue subtitles are derived from excerpts of full-movie scripts, and our\n852\tchallenge set is based on full movies — we share these after researchers agree to terms of\n853\tuse. We release code necessary to evaluate future AD generation systems with the metrics\n854\tproposed in this work.\n855\tOur dataset only represents English AD, since it forms the largest available data. In our\n856\tcompanion paper (Sterner et al., 2026), we test hypotheses concerning the differences\n857\tbetween American and British AD (for results separated by references, see Appendix\n858\tTable 19). We expect many aspects of the formulation and evaluation setup to transfer\n859\tcross-lingually. Professionally authored AD in many languages follows similar constraints\n860\tregarding dialogue gaps and narrative accessibility. On the other hand, stylistic conventions\n861\tvary across regions. Independently created AD tracks already exist in many languages\n862\tfor a subset of our movies, which could enable future cross-lingual extensions using our\n863\tmethodology.\n864\t\n865\tAcknowledgements\n866\tSterner is funded by an EPSRC PhD studentship (project reference 2923920). Lapata gratefully acknowledges the support of the UK Engineering and Physical Sciences Research\n867\tCouncil (grant EP/W002876/1). We thank Arian Merati for his contributions to the evaluation of AD transcription and diarization. We are grateful to the team at Speechmatics\n868\tfor providing API access to their models and Google for providing Gemini API credits.\n869\tWe thank Inderjeet Mani, Argyrios Papoudakis, Olga Loginova, and three anonymous\n870\treviewers for their constructive feedback on this manuscript.\n871\t\n872\tReferences\n873\tApoorv Agarwal, Sriramkumar Balasubramanian, Jiehan Zheng, and Sarthak Dash. Parsing screenplays for extracting social networks from movies. In Anna Feldman, Anna\n874\tKazantseva, and Stan Szpakowicz (eds.), Proceedings of the Workshop on Computational\n875\tLinguistics for Literature (CLFL), pp. 50–58, Gothenburg, Sweden, April 2014. Association\n876\tfor Computational Linguistics. URL https://doi.org/10.3115/v1/W14-0907.\n877\tPeter Anderson, Basura Fernando, Mark Johnson, and Stephen Gould. SPICE: Semantic\n878\tpropositional image caption evaluation. In Proceedings of the European Conference on\n879\tComputer Vision (ECCV), pp. 382–398. Springer, 2016. URL https://doi.org/10.1007/\n880\t978-3-319-46454-1 24.\n881\tMax Bain, Arsha Nagrani, Andrew Brown, and Andrew Zisserman. Condensed Movies:\n882\tStory b