\tChao-Yuan Wu and Philipp Krahenbuhl. Towards long-form video understanding. In\n1148\tProceedings of the IEEE/CVF conference on computer vision and pattern recognition, pp. 1884–\n1149\t1894, 2021. URL https://doi.org/10.1109/CVPR46437.2021.00192.\n1150\t16\n1151\t\n1152\t\fPublished as a conference paper at COLM 2026\n1153\t\n1154\tHaoning Wu, Dongxu Li, Bei Chen, and Junnan Li. LongVideoBench: A benchmark for\n1155\tlong-context interleaved video-language understanding. In Proceedings of the International\n1156\tConference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS), 2024. URL https://dl.acm.\n1157\torg/doi/10.5555/3737916.3738823.\n1158\tJunyu Xie, Tengda Han, Max Bain, Arsha Nagrani, Gül Varol, Weidi Xie, and Andrew\n1159\tZisserman. AutoAD-Zero: A training-free framework for zero-shot audio description. In\n1160\tProceedings of the Asian Conference on Computer Vision (ACCV), pp. 2265–2281, December\n1161\t2024. URL https://doi.org/10.1007/978-981-96-0908-6 5.\n1162\tJunyu Xie, Tengda Han, Max Bain, Arsha Nagrani, Eshika Khandelwal, Gül Varol, Weidi\n1163\tXie, and Andrew Zisserman. Shot-by-Shot: Film-grammar-aware training-free audio\n1164\tdescription generation. In Proceedings of the International Conference on Computer Vision\n1165\t(ICCV), pp. 16503–16513, October 2025. URL https://doi.org/10.1109/ICCV51701.2025.\n1166\t01532.\n1167\tXiaojun Ye, Junhao Chen, Xiang Li, Haidong Xin, Chao Li, Sheng Zhou, and Jiajun Bu.\n1168\tMMAD: Multi-modal movie audio description. In Nicoletta Calzolari, Min-Yen Kan,\n1169\tVeronique Hoste, Alessandro Lenci, Sakriani Sakti, and Nianwen Xue (eds.), Proceedings\n1170\tof the Joint International Conference on Computational Linguistics, Language Resources and\n1171\tEvaluation (LREC-COLING), pp. 11415–11428, Torino, Italia, May 2024. ELRA and ICCL.\n1172\tURL https://aclanthology.org/2024.lrec-main.998.\n1173\tXiaojun Ye, Chun Wang, Yiren Song, Sheng Zhou, Liangcheng Li, and Jiajun Bu. FocusedAD:\n1174\tCharacter-centric movie audio description, 2025. URL https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.\n1175\t2504.12157.\n1176\tZihao Yue, Qi Zhang, Anwen Hu, Liang Zhang, Ziheng Wang, and Qin Jin. Movie101:\n1177\tA new movie understanding benchmark. In Anna Rogers, Jordan Boyd-Graber, and\n1178\tNaoaki Okazaki (eds.), Proceedings of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL),\n1179\tpp. 4669–4684, Toronto, Canada, July 2023. Association for Computational Linguistics.\n1180\tURL https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2023.acl-long.257.\n1181\tZihao Yue, Yepeng Zhang, Ziheng Wang, and Qin Jin. Movie101v2: Improved movie\n1182\tnarration benchmark. In Wanxiang Che, Joyce Nabende, Ekaterina Shutova, and Mohammad Taher Pilehvar (eds.), Proceedings of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL),\n1183\tpp. 17081–17095, Vienna, Austria, July 2025. Association for Computational Linguistics.\n1184\tISBN 979-8-89176-251-0. URL https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2025.acl-long.836.\n1185\tEmmanouil Zaranis, António Farinhas, Saul Santos, Beatriz Canaverde, Miguel Moura\n1186\tRamos, Aditya K Surikuchi, André Viveiros, Baohao Liao, Elena Bueno-Benito, Nithin\n1187\tSivakumaran, Pavlo Vasylenko, Shoubin Yu, Sonal Sannigrahi, Wafaa Mohammed, Ben\n1188\tPeters, Danae Sánchez Villegas, Elias Stengel-Eskin, Giuseppe Attanasio, Jaehong Yoon,\n1189\tStella Frank, Alessandro Suglia, Chrysoula Zerva, Desmond Elliott, Mariella Dimiccoli,\n1190\tMohit Bansal, Oswald Lanz, Raffaella Bernardi, Raquel Fernández, Sandro Pezzelle, Vlad\n1191\tNiculae, and André F. T. Martins. Movie facts and fibs (MF2 ): A benchmark for long\n1192\tmovie understanding, 2025. URL https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2506.06275.\n1193\tChaoyi Zhang, Kevin Lin, Zhengyuan Yang, Jianfeng Wang, Linjie Li, Chung-Ching Lin,\n1194\tZicheng Liu, and Lijuan Wang. MM-Narrator: Narrating long-form videos with multimodal in-context learning. In Proceedings of the Conference on Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (CVPR), pp. 13647–13657, June 2024. URL https://doi.org/10.1109/\n1195\tCVPR52733.2024.01295.\n1196\tTianyi Zhang, Varsha Kishore, Felix Wu, Kilian Q. Weinberger, and Yoav Artzi. BERTScore:\n1197\tEvaluating text generation with BERT. In Proceedings of the International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR), 2020. URL https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.1904.09675.\n1198\tYanzhao Zhang, Mingxin Li, Dingkun Long, Xin Zhang, Huan Lin, Baosong Yang, Pengjun\n1199\tXie, An Yang, Dayiheng Liu, Junyang Lin, Fei Huang, and Jingren Zhou. Qwen3 Embedding: Advancing text embedding and reranking through foundation models, 2025. URL\n1200\thttps://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2506.05176.\n1201\t17\n1202\t\n1203\t\fPublished as a conference paper at COLM 2026\n1204\t\n1205\tLianmin Zheng, Wei-Lin Chiang, Ying Sheng, Siyuan Zhuang, Zhanghao Wu, Yonghao\n1206\tZhuang, Zi Lin, Zhuohan Li, Dacheng Li, Eric P. Xing, Hao Zhang, Joseph E. Gonzalez,\n1207\tand Ion Stoica. Judging LLM-as-a-judge with MT-bench and Chatbot Arena. In Proceedings\n1208\tof the International Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS), Red Hook,\n1209\tNY, USA, 2023. Curran Associates Inc. URL https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.5555/3666122.\n1210\t3668142.\n1211\t\n1212\t18\n1213\t\n1214\t\fPublished as a conference paper at COLM 2026\n1215\t\n1216\tA\n1217\t\n1218\tOther Datasets and Evaluation Metrics\n1219\t\n1220\tOther Datasets. Other datasets also exist, such as TV-AD (Xie et al., 2024), YouDescribe\n1221\t(Pitcher-Cooper et al., 2024) (as used by Oncescu et al. (2021)) and Movie101 v1 and v2 (Yue\n1222\tet al., 2023; 2025). Datasets of movie summaries, also known as recaps, exist (Dogan et al.,\n1223\t2018; Sun et al., 2025; 2024; Lu et al., 2024). Our choice to work with AD for entire movies\n1224\tis based on the fact that they are self-contained and have long-form, complex narratives.\n1225\tDatasets of egocentric video with parallel descriptions are similar to AD in providing realtime narration of visual content. Epic Kitchens 100 (Damen et al., 2022) and HowTo100M\n1226\t(Miech et al., 2019) are two examples. These corpora are specifically designed to tackle\n1227\taction recognition, which is not equivalent to the task of generating AD.\n1228\tEvaluation. Table 3 gives, to the best of our knowledge, an exhaustive summary of metrics\n1229\tthat have been applied to the previous task of AD as video captioning. Rows (1)–(4) are\n1230\tvariants of n-gram metrics, (5)–(6) were designed for image captioning using semantic\n1231\tscene parses or neural embeddings, (7)–(8) are tailored to report on repetition and character\n1232\tnaming in AD, (9–11) use LLMs, either generation or encoder models, (12) addresses only\n1233\tverbs in generated AD, and (13)–(14) are an existing QA evaluation setup for AD.\n1234\tMetric\n1235\t\n1236\tFirst proposed by\n1237\t\n1238\tFor AD proposed by\n1239\t\n1240\t(1) BLEU\n1241\t\n1242\tPapineni et al. (2002)\n1243\t\n1244\tRohrbach et al. (2015)\n1245\t\n1246\tDescription\n1247\t\n1248\tn-gram precision + brevity\n1249\tpenalty\n1250\t(2) METEOR\n1251\tBanerjee & Lavie (2005) Rohrbach et al. (2017) Fmean of unigram with stem\n1252\tand synonym matching +\n1253\tfragmentation penalty.\n1254\t(3) ROUGE-L\n1255\tLin (2004)\n1256\tHan et al. (2023a)\n1257\tF1 of longest common subsequence\n1258\t(4) CIDEr\n1259\tVedantam et al. (2015)\n1260\tHan et al. (2023a)\n1261\tCosine similarity of TF-IDF\n1262\ton n-grams\n1263\t(5) SPICE\n1264\tAnderson et al. (2016)\n1265\tHan et al. (2023a)\n1266\tF1 of a semantic graph\n1267\t(6) BERT-Score\n1268\tZhang et al. (2020)\n1269\tHan et al. (2023a)\n1270\tF1 of BERT embeddings\n1271\t(7) Recall@k/N\n1272\t—\n1273\tHan et al. (2023b)\n1274\tRank window-N references\n1275\tby BERT-score and check if\n1276\tthe gold pair is in top-k\n1277\tProportion of reference char(8) Character names\n1278\t—\n1279\tHan et al. (2024)\n1280\tacters mentioned\n1281\tPrompt an LLM for a score in\n1282\t(9) LLM-as-a-Judge Zheng et al. (2023)\n1283\tHan et al. (2024)\n1284\t[0, 5]\n1285\t(10) LLM-as-a-Judge\n1286\tVenugopalan et al. (2025) Prompt an LLM for scores\n1287\t(via AD guidelines)\n1288\taccording to criteria in AD\n1289\tguidelines\n1290\t(11) Sentence similarity\n1291\tXie et al. (2025)\n1292\tMax sentence similarity in a\n1293\twindow\n1294\t(12) Verb lemmas\n1295\tXie et al. (2025)\n1296\tProportion of reference verbs\n1297\tmentioned\n1298\t(13) Fact-based QA\n1299\tKala et al. (2025)\n1300\tMultiple-choice QA using\n1301\tLLMs + correct rationale\n1302\t(14) Narrative-based\n1303\tKala et al. (2025)\n1304\tAs above, but based on\n1305\tQA\n1306\taligned plot summary sentences\n1307\t\n1308\tTable 3: Evaluation metrics for AD generation. To the best of our knowledge, an exhaustive\n1309\tlist of metrics previously applied to AD (framed as video captioning), grouped by type:\n1310\tn-gram overlap (1–4), image-captioning (5–6), AD-specific repetition and character naming\n1311\t(7–8), LLM-based (9–11), verb coverage (12), and QA-based (13–14). First proposed by gives\n1312\teach metric’s origin; For AD proposed by gives the first work to apply it to AD.\n1313\t\n1314\t19\n1315\t\n1316\t\fPublished as a conference paper at COLM 2026\n1317\t\n1318\tB\n1319\t\n1320\tProfessional AD Transcription\n1321\t\n1322\tFor AD transcription in the evaluation sets of Re frame d and for our use for evaluating\n1323\tdata collection techniques, we contracted the work of a movie post-production company.\n1324\tThey performed fully manual AD transcription and timecoding to the nearest frame (approximately 0.04 seconds). The delivery consists of segments of transcription, each with a\n1325\tstart and end timecode. One segment was defined as a single event, action or other visual\n1326\telement being described (they are frequently sentence-fragments).\n1327\tWe chose a company with experience with feature-film subtitling and AD, and one that\n1328\tassured us that they do not use any automatic AI tools in their work. For each movie that\n1329\twe provided them, the company researches the movie and the spellings of particularly hard\n1330\tcharacters and movie-related words. The timestamps are manually determined based on\n1331\tclicking between individual frames in the video and listening to when the audio begins.\n1332\tThe data went through a full round of professional quality control from the company.\n1333\t\n1334\tC\n1335\t\n1336\tEvaluating Pre-existing Data Collection Techniques\n1337\t\n1338\tHere we evaluate the effectiveness of pre-existing data collection techniques. The first task\n1339\tis for a system to take as input a movie-length AD track, and output a transcription of it\n1340\t(text of what was said) and diarization (speaker labels for each span within the transcription;\n1341\tneeded to differentiate character dialogue from the AD narrator). The second task is for a\n1342\tsystem to take as input a short movie clip and a full-length AD movie track, and output the\n1343\toffset and speedup of the clip with respect to the full track.\n1344\tIn order to define the first task, we need a gold-standard AD transcription. For the second\n1345\ttask, we need aligned full-length movie and AD tracks, and then we can create evaluation data by extracting segments from the movie track and applying speed changes. For\n1346\tthese evaluations, we used a newly transcribed version of MAD-v2-eval, which we call\n1347\tMAD-v3-eval. We purchased the 10 movies in this evaluation set. Figure 3 on page 6\n1348\tshows the differences between MAD-v2-eval and MAD-v3-eval timestamps. A positive\n1349\tdifference indicates that the AD described the event before it occurred, and vice-versa. A\n1350\tGaussian best fit shows that on average AD describes events 0.8 seconds in advance, and\n1351\tthe standard deviation is 2.4 seconds. This spread demonstrates that a central part of the\n1352\ttask of creating AD is identifying suitable gaps in which to place a description that is nearby\n1353\tto its corresponding visual content; the visual event being described is not necessarily at\n1354\texactly at the same time as when it is described in the AD.\n1355\tC.1\n1356\t\n1357\tEvaluating Automatic AD Transcription.\n1358\t\n1359\tWe compare the capabilities of three ASR systems on the task of transcribing AD: WhisperX\n1360\t(Bain et al., 2023) as used for the CMD-AD data; industry APIs from Microsoft (as used\n1361\tfor the MAD training data); and Speechmatics (a reputable company that specialises in\n1362\tASR). We can also evaluate the quality of the MAD-v2-eval transcripts against our new\n1363\treference. Characters play a central role in movie narratives, and hence it is vital that they\n1364\tare transcribed correctly. We apply pre-existing methods for guiding ASR models to spell\n1365\tparticular words correctly, which for us is character names. For more details, see Section F.\n1366\tResults are in Table 4. MAD-v2-eval achieves overall WER of 22.1%, largely as a result of a\n1367\thigh deletion rate: evidence that this data is of insufficient quality. WhisperX is better with a\n1368\tWER of 12.8%. Across all metrics, the Speechmatics systems perform best with overall WER\n1369\tof 2.9%. The good overall performance can be traced back, in part, to excellent diarization\n1370\tperformance. They also provide the only systems with ≤1% timestamp error rate within 0.2\n1371\tseconds. With our character adaptation, it spells ≤5% of character names incorrectly.\n1372\tC.2\n1373\t\n1374\tEvaluating Temporal Alignment of Scenes to Whole Movies.\n1375\t\n1376\tThe task now is to temporally identify where in a full-length AD track a given movie scene\n1377\tcame from. This task is made more challenging in practice because of the speed differences\n1378\t20\n1379\t\n1380\t\fPublished as a conference paper at COLM 2026\n1381\t\n1382\tASR\n1383\t\n1384\tDiarization\n1385\t\n1386\tSR DR IR WER DER MR FAR\n1387\t\n1388\tNames\n1389\t\n1390\tTimes\n1391\t\n1392