 or 0.4 seconds. Overall: end-to-end WER (and SR/DR/IR) after automatic diarization.\n1521\tThe lower block repeats the systems with character-name adaptation. See Appendix F and\n1522\tTables 11–12 for full details and all system variants.\n1523\t\n1524\t0\n1525\t0.90 0.91 0.92 0.93 0.94 0.95 0.96 0.97 0.98 0.99 1.00 1.01 1.02 1.03 1.04\n1526\t\n1527\tSpeedup\n1528\tFigure 5: A histogram of the speedups computed by Han et al. (2024) for CMD-AD.\n1529\t\n1530\tcaused by the different frame rates of the two main standards: NTSC (as used in North\n1531\tAmerica, which uses 24000/1001 frames per second) and PAL (as used in Europe, which\n1532\tuses 25 frames per second). In order to treat potential speed differences, all we need to do\n1533\tis allow for one version to be NTSC and the other PAL, or both are NTSC/PAL. Figure 5\n1534\tshows reverse-engineered data released by Han et al. (2024) — experimentally we find that\n1535\ttheir predicted speedups are consistent with these two theoretical results.\n1536\tHence our task definition is: given a full-length AD track and a movie scene, identify the time of the start of the movie scene in the AD track and determine if the\n1537\tscene needs a PAL-NTSC speeddown, no speed change, or a NTSC-PAL speedup.\n1538\tWe create many samples of each case by using\n1539\tthe pre-aligned full movies in MAD-v3-eval, and\n1540\tParameter\n1541\tValue\n1542\twe do so for many different lengths of movie\n1543\tTarget sample rate\n1544\t16000\n1545\tscene. Han et al.’s (2024) RANSAC-based method\n1546\tNumber of Mel bands\n1547\t128\n1548\tcan directly be applied to the task; for evaluaFFT\n1549\twindow\n1550\tsize\n1551\t1024\n1552\ttion, we consider any predicted speed difference\n1553\tHop length\n1554\t512\n1555\twithin 0.01 of the reference to be correct. Soldan\n1556\tRANSAC window size\n1557\t50\n1558\tet al.’s (2022) cross-correlation can only predict\n1559\tthe offset, so we adapt it by repeating the crosscorrelation computation for each of the possible Table 5: Hyperparameters for audio\n1560\tspeed changes and use the one with highest cor- feature extraction and cross-correlation\n1561\trelation score to be the prediction. We consider alignment.\n1562\toffsets to be correct if they are within 0.04 seconds\n1563\t21\n1564\t\n1565\t\fPublished as a conference paper at COLM 2026\n1566\t\n1567\t80\n1568\t\n1569\tSpeedup accuracy (%)\n1570\t\n1571\tOffset accuracy (%)\n1572\t\n1573\t100\n1574\tC ROSS - CORRELATION\n1575\tRANSAC\n1576\t\n1577\t60\n1578\t40\n1579\t20\n1580\t0\n1581\t\n1582\t10−3 10−2 10−1 100\n1583\t\n1584\t101\n1585\t\n1586\t102\n1587\t\n1588\t100\n1589\t80\n1590\t60\n1591\t40\n1592\t20\n1593\t0\n1594\t\n1595\t103\n1596\t\n1597\tClip duration (s)\n1598\t\n1599\t10−3 10−2 10−1 100\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"28a31d0a-d593-4419-87c8-544579cdfc2c","uuid":"e3b1d5eb-31f9-480a-b90d-3b6331c10181","timestamp":"2026-08-11T12:11:26.439Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/reframed.txt","content":"dataset for vision-language story understanding. In Yaser Al-Onaizan, Mohit Bansal, and\nYun-Nung Chen (eds.), Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: EMNLP,\npp. 13488–13504, Miami, Florida, USA, November 2024. Association for Computational\nLinguistics. URL https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2024.findings-emnlp.788.\nYidan Sun, Qin Chao, Yangfeng Ji, and Boyang Li. Synopses of movie narratives: a videolanguage dataset for story understanding. In Proceedings of the International Conference on\nMultimedia and Expo (ICME), pp. 1–6, 2025. URL https://doi.org/10.1109/ICME59968.\n2025.11209961.\nMakarand Tapas