{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-916d4ec28086037f","type":"tool_result","content":"1600\t\n1601\t101\n1602\t\n1603\t102\n1604\t\n1605\t103\n1606\t\n1607\tClip duration (s)\n1608\t\n1609\t(a) Accuracy of determining movie scene offset.\n1610\t\n1611\t(b) Accuracy of determining the speed difference.\n1612\t\n1613\tFigure 6: Scene-to-movie temporal alignment accuracy as a function of scene duration,\n1614\tcomparing cross-correlation (Soldan et al., 2022) against the RANSAC-based method of\n1615\tHan et al. (2024). Cross-correlation reaches perfect accuracy for scenes of ≥32 seconds,\n1616\twhile the RANSAC method is reliable only at much longer durations; we therefore adopt\n1617\tcross-correlation in Re frame d.\n1618\tof the reference. Table 5 details the specific hyperparameters used for feature extraction and\n1619\talignment.\n1620\tFigure 6 provides results. We can see that the success of the methods depends greatly on\n1621\tthe length of the scene to be aligned. The only approach to achieve perfect performance\n1622\tis using cross-correlation on scenes of at least 32 seconds. The RANSAC-based method\n1623\tperforms poorly without very long scene durations. Our experiment here also suggests why\n1624\tthe RANSAC method is inferior—it relies at its core on applying many cross-correlation\n1625\tto 3 second windows, and we have shown here that cross-correlation is very noisy at this\n1626\ttime-scale.\n1627\tIn Re frame d, we opt to use cross-correlation. In order to guarantee the highest possible\n1628\tquality assurance, we additionally require that all the offsets for a video from a given movie\n1629\timpute an OLS regression line between the US and UK AD tracks with RMSE ≤ 1. We state\n1630\tthat we have high confidence in the alignment of the resulting videos based on the fact that\n1631\tit would be extremely unlikely for ≥5 independent alignment offsets to maintain linear\n1632\tconsistency across the entire movie if one or more was erroneous.\n1633\t\n1634\tD\n1635\t\n1636\tChallenge Set Annotation\n1637\t\n1638\tThe data in the Re frame d challenge set of full movies serves both as an evaluation for AD\n1639\tgeneration and as an evaluation material for further data collection steps that we perform.\n1640\tAnnotation for this set was performed manually.\n1641\tHigh-level Overview. There are 10 movies, and each movie is provided with 2-3 AD\n1642\ttranscripts. The movies are either 2023/2024 movies with award winning AD tracks (2/10),\n1643\thave three independently-created AD versions (1/10), or are the same movies as in MADv3-eval (7/10). The exact frame that starts each new scene in every movie is labeled. Each\n1644\tAD segment is labeled with a label of the scene whose content it describes. The screenplays\n1645\tscenes are mapped to the movie scenes. Boundaries between sequences of scenes are\n1646\tobtained from the official movie release chapter boundaries. English SDH and English\n1647\tdialogue subtitles are from the official movie release.\n1648\tDetails. For each movie in our benchmark, we require the availability of independently\n1649\tcreated US and UK AD versions for use as multiple references. For seven out of the ten\n1650\tmovies in MAD-v3-eval, we were able to identify and purchase movies with the other AD\n1651\t22\n1652\t\n1653\t\fPublished as a conference paper at COLM 2026\n1654\t\n1655\tversion to the version in the previous data. We add two movies which received awards for\n1656\tthe quality of their AD tracks in the AD Awards gala and/or EGA Hermes Awards and also\n1657\thave US and UK AD versions, namely Dune Part Two (2024) and Barbie (2023). We add a\n1658\tfinal movie for which we were able to obtain three independent AD tracks (two US versions,\n1659\tone UK version), namely Priscilla (2023). The benchmark now contains movies that span\n1660\ttwo decades (2005–2024) and a wide range of genres.\n1661\tWe contracted the film post-production company to provide us with professional AD transcripts for each of the 21 (9 movies with two references, 1 movie with three references)\n1662\ttracks. An annotator manually determined if PAL–NTSC speedups and/or offsets are required between the versions and ensure that all versions are temporally aligned, determined\n1663\tthe scene boundaries in the movie and determined which AD segments correspond to\n1664\twhich scene. The process was as follows: (1) using professional subtitling software, we\n1665\tlabeled the exact frame when each scene changes, following a strict definition of scene\n1666\tchanges as a change of location or time, (2) using these boundaries, the movie video, and\n1667\tthe timecoded AD, we label the scene to which each AD segment is describing content.\n1668\tScreenplays for 4/10 of the movies are provided by MovieSum (Saxena & Keller, 2024), and\n1669\tfor the remaining 6 we follow Saxena & Keller’s (2024) parsing method to obtain parsed\n1670\tscreenplays, which involves full manual review and correction. The annotator manually\n1671\tlabels the alignment between the movie scenes and the scenes in the screenplays from all\n1672\tten movies. There was no access to the screenplay to do the scene segmentation, as a result\n1673\tone-to-many and many-to-one alignments are possible. Chapter boundaries are provided\n1674\tdirectly by our purchased versions of the movies, which we use as sequence boundaries.\n1675\tWe obtain professional dialogue subtitles by applying OCR (Tesseract 5.5.0) to the official\n1676\tmovie subtitles and manually reviewing (using Subtitle Edit software). We apply this to the\n1677\tEnglish SDH and English dialogue subtitles, or if only the former is available (4/10 cases)\n1678\tthen we manually edit the SDH version to remove text other than the dialogue subtitles.\n1679\tThe only potentially subjective task of the above is determining scene boundaries from the\n1680\tmovie. We do not perform any double-annotation and hence cannot report inter-annotator\n1681\tagreement on the task. One of the movies in our data is also provided as a part of MovieNet\n1682\t(Huang et al., 2020), which provides data that is the established benchmark for movie scene\n1683\tsegmentation. The MovieNet annotation of scene segmentation is crowd-sourced and based\n1684\ton the decisions of an annotator (who likely has not watched the full movies) provided\n1685\taccess only to stills from neighbouring shots (determined via automatic shot detection). For\n1686\ta movie in both datasets, Les Miserables (2012), we were surprised to find their annotation\n1687\treports 228 scenes, while ours has only 113. We inspected their boundaries and found them\n1688\tto consistently oversegment, in particular in settings when neighbouring shots are visually\n1689\tdifferent but in fact just a different view of the same scene. As an independent reference, the\n1690\tscreenplay of this movie has 133 scenes (some of which were likely removed for the final\n1691\tversion).\n1692\t\n1693\tE\n1694\t\n1695\tNew Data Collection Techniques\n1696\t\n1697\tWorking with screenplays brings challenges, both in terms of parsing the PDFs available of\n1698\tthem and aligning them to the final movie.\n1699\tScreenplays are released as 100+ page PDFs, with a reasonably standard scene-segmented\n1700\tstructure that is displayed via indentations and font formatting. The task of parsing such\n1701\tdocuments into their structured contents has been addressed by prior work (Turetsky &\n1702\tDimitrova, 2004; Agarwal et al., 2014; Winer & Young, 2017). However, the task remains\n1703\tchallenging due to different formatting styles (Gil et al., 2011) and in practice several hours\n1704\tof human supervision is needed per movie for perfect parsing (Saxena & Keller, 2024). We\n1705\tdo not tackle this task and instead rely on the gold-standard machine-parsed screenplays\n1706\tfrom the MovieSum dataset (Saxena & Keller, 2024).\n1707\tHowever, we found current alignment methods to be insufficient for our purposes. In\n1708\tour approach to screenplay alignment, we define the task at the scene-level as follows:\n1709\tgiven S = {si }iS=1 scenes from a machine-parseable screenplay and the dialogue and AD\n1710\t23\n1711\t\n1712\t\fPublished as a conference paper at COLM 2026\n1713\t\n1714\tfrom M = {m j } jM=1 scenes in the final movie, compute the alignment A ⊆ S × M. The\n1715\tscreenplay’s structured format provides exact segmentation into the S scenes, but scene\n1716\tsegmentation is not available for the final movie. Our insight here is that AD will frequently\n1717\tcue movie scene changes; this is a requirement stipulated in both American and British AD\n1718\tguidelines. If we can detect such cues, then we have a high-precision and scalable method\n1719\tof determining movie scene boundaries.\n1720\tE.1\n1721\t\n1722\tMovie-Screenplay Alignment\n1723\t\n1724\tEvaluation and Baselines. Our evaluation metric for movie-screenplay alignment is\n1725\tweighted F1 score, where the weights are based on the lengths of the scenes in the movie. We\n1726\twill compare our methods against the previous state-of-the-art: aligning a movie’s dialogue\n1727\tsubtitles with dialogue in the movie’s screenplay by computing a dynamic time warping\n1728\t(DTW) over word tokens. In this baseline, we assign any screenplay dialogue word with an\n1729\texact match to a movie dialogue word the scene the movie word is from.\n1730\tDTW over word matches is frequently applied for the task of movie-screenplay alignment\n1731\t(Everingham et al., 2006; Sivic et al., 2009; Bouritsas et al., 2018; Papalampidi et al., 2021).\n1732\tNevertheless, results from Lambert et al. (2013) suggest that DTW may not be the ideal\n1733\talgorithm, because it favours on-diagonal matches in the alignment. This is a particular\n1734\tproblem for screenplay drafts before a post-production or even shooting version. Lambert et al. (2013) propose to upweight the score that results from matches; this is akin to\n1735\tminimizing edit distance, which we will employ as a second baseline.2\n1736\tVecalign-based Alignment. In our approach, we compute embeddings for each scene in\n1737\tthe movie and each scene in the screenplay, and then use dynamic programming to align\n1738\tthe two. We additionally treat the potential for a screenplay scene to have been removed\n1739\tat production, or for a new scene to be added at production. Thompson & Koehn (2019)\n1740\tprovide methods designed for compiling machine translation corpora that can be of use\n1741\there, which they call Vecalign. In particular they define a cost in the alignment algorithm\n1742\twhich allows scenes to be skipped in either the movie or the screenplay, without alignment.\n1743\tWe use a fixed skip cost cskip = 0.4. Vecalign also treats one-to-many and many-to-one\n1744\talignments by allowing for merge operations between contiguous sequences of scenes. We\n1745\tuse mean-pooling as the merge operation. We allow for alignment blocks of up to 10 scenes.\n1746\tVecalign also allows for, albeit discourages in the cost function, many-to-many alignments;\n1747\twe disallow these via search constraints so that the final alignment is strictly monotonic\n1748\tover the movie scenes. Vecalign also incorporates methods that reduce the alignment time\n1749\tcomplexity from quadratic to linear via a recursive approximation; we do not apply these\n1750\tmethods and optimize over the full alignment.\n1751\tThe cost c(s, m) of aligning a candidate block of screenplay scenes s ⊆ S to a candidate\n1752\tblock of movie scenes m ⊆ M is defined as:\n1753\t\u0001\n1754\t1 − cos(s̄, m̄) · |s| · |m|\n1755\tc(s, m) = 1\n1756\t(1)\n1757\t\u0001\n1758\t\u0001\n1759\tM\n1760\tS\n1761\t1\n1762\t2M ∑ j=1 1 − cos( s̄, m j ) + 2S ∑i =1 1 − cos( si , m̄ )\n1763\twhere boldface denotes scene embeddings, bar notation denotes embeddings after meanpooling, and | · | denotes the number of scenes in a block. The numerator scales the similarity\n1764\tcost by the number of scenes in the block alignment, and the denominator normalizes by\n1765\tthe mean similarity between the candidate block and all scenes.\n1766\tWith initialization D (0, 0) = 0, D (i, 0) = i · cskip , and D (0, j) = j · cskip , the dynamic\n1767\tprogramming recurrence relation is as follows:\n1768\t\n1769\tD (i − 1, j) + cskip\n1770\t(skip screenplay scene)\n1771\t\n1772\t\n1773\t\n1774\tD\n1775\t(\n1776\ti,\n1777\tj\n1778\t−\n1779\t1\n1780\t)\n1781\t+\n1782\tc\n1783\t(skip movie scene)\n1784\tskip\n1785\tD (i, j) = min\n1786\th\n1787\t\u0001i\n1788\t\n1789\t\n1790\t(block match)\n1791\t min D (i − |s|, j − |m|) + c Si−|s|:i , M j−|m|:j\n1792\t| s |,| m |\n1793\t\n1794\t2 We use the implementation from https://github.com/jitsi/jiwer\n1795\t\n1796\t24\n1797\t\n1798\t\fPublished as a conference paper at COLM 2026\n1799\t\n1800\tModel\n1801\t\n1802\tP\n1803\t\n1804\tR\n1805\t\n1806\tF1\n1807\t\n1808\t—\n1809\t—\n1810\t\n1811\t63.7\n1812\t66.4\n1813\t\n1814\t62.4\n1815\t63.1\n1816\t\n1817\t62.3\n1818\t63.8\n1819\t\n1820\tdialogue\n1821\tSDH\n1822\tUK AD\n1823\tUS AD\n1824\tUK AD + US AD\n1825\tdialogue + UK AD\n1826\tSDH + UK AD\n1827\tdialogue + US AD\n1828\tSDH + US AD\n1829\tdialogue + UK AD + US AD\n1830\t\n1831\tQwen3 E 8B\n1832\tQwen3 E 8B\n1833\tQwen3 E 8B\n1834\tQwen3 E 8B\n1835\tQwen3 E 8B\n1836\tQwen3 E 8B\n1837\tQwen3 E 8B\n1838\tQwen3 E 8B\n1839\tQwen3 E 8B\n1840\tQwen3 E 8B\n1841\t\n1842\t81.4\n1843\t82.8\n1844\t76.9\n1845\t77.0\n1846\t76.9\n1847\t82.1\n1848\t83.6\n1849\t84.7\n1850\t85.1\n1851\t83.1\n1852\t\n1853\t53.4\n1854\t58.5\n1855\t67.1\n1856\t70.0\n1857\t72.8\n1858\t73.7\n1859\t73.1\n1860\t73.9\n1861\t73.9\n1862\t77.4\n1863\t\n1864\t64.2\n1865\t68.0\n1866\t71.3\n1867\t73.0\n1868\t74.5\n1869\t77.3\n1870\t77.5\n1871\t78.6\n1872\t78.7\n1873\t79.9\n1874\t\n1875\tSDH + UK AD + US AD\n1876\tSDH + UK AD + US AD\n1877\tSDH + UK AD + US AD\n1878\t\n1879\tQwen3 E 0.6B\n1880\tQwen3 E 4B\n1881\tQwen3 E 8B\n1882\t\n1883\t87.5\n1884\t85.3\n1885\t84.9\n1886\t\n1887\t62.4\n1888\t73.8\n1889\t77.7\n1890\t\n1891\t71.8\n1892\t78.8\n1893\t80.9\n1894\t\n1895\tMethod\n1896\t\n1897\tData\n1898\t\n1899\tText-based DTW\n1900\tText-based MED\n1901\t\n1902\tdialogue\n1903\tdialogue\n1904\t\n1905\tVecalign\n1906\tVecalign\n1907\tVecalign\n1908\tVecalign\n1909\tVecalign\n1910\tVecalign\n1911\tVecalign\n1912\tVecalign\n1913\tVecalign\n1914\tVecalign\n1915\tVecalign\n1916\tVecalign\n1917\tVecalign\n1918\t\n1919\tTable 6: Screenplay alignment results. DTW = dynamic time warping, MED = minimum\n1920\tedit distance. Text-based baselines are in first block, ablation results are in second block,\n1921\tand best results based on embedding models of three sizes are in final block.\n1922\t\n1923\twhere the search space is constrained such that min(|s|, |m|) = 1 and |s| + |m| ≤ 10. The\n1924\tfinal alignment A is then recovered by backtracking along the minimal-cost path from\n1925\tD (S, M ) to D (0, 0) and collecting the matched scenes.\n1926\tEmbeddings. For the movies, we will define the embeddings based on a time-sorted\n1927\tconcatenation of the subtitles (SDH or dialogue-only) and/or AD (UK and/or US versions)\n1928\tin each scene in our gold-standard annotation; for the screenplay we define the embedding\n1929\tbased on the dialogue and scene descriptions in each scene in the order that they appear.\n1930\tEmbeddings are computed according to Zhang et al. (2025).\n1931\tResults. Results are in Table 6. The word-based DTW baseline achieves F1 = 62.3, and the\n1932\tminimum edit distance variant achieves F1 = 63.8. Performance with Vecalign applied to\n1933\tthe same dialogue is comparable at F1 = 64.2. Using instead SDH leads to an improved F1 =\n1934\t68.0 (+3.8), demonstrating that the non-verbal audio captions contain useful information\n1935\tfor the alignment. We can outperform these dialogue-based alignment approaches using\n1936\tonly one of UK or US AD (F1 = 71.3 and F1 = 73.0), demonstrating that AD also contains\n1937\tuseful information for the alignment. Using both AD versions results in better performance\n1938\tthan using only one (F1 = 74.5, +3.2 and +1.5 for AD versions). (Table 6 also gives results\n1939\tfor other combinations of input data.) Our best results (F1 =80.9) are based on using SDH\n1940\tand both AD versions with Vecalign. In our experiments so far, we have used the Qwen3\n1941\tEmbedding 8B model for computing the embeddings. Using the 4B or 0.6B variants of this\n1942\tmodel leads to worse performance (F1 =78.8 and F1 =71.8, respectively). We apply this best\n1943\talignment pipeline to all movies with screenplays in Re frame d.\n1944\tE.2\n1945\t\n1946\tMovie Scene Segmentation from AD\n1947\t\n1948\tAD scene segmentation. We detect scene change cues in AD by finetuning RoBERTalarge (Liu et al., 2019) on our manually annotated data. The task is for a model to classify\n1949\teach segment of AD as starting a new scene or not, given the context of surrounding AD\n1950\tsegments. Our evaluation metric is binary F1 score. We perform 10-fold cross-validation\n1951\tand report the macro-average. Our baseline will assume that all AD segments that begin\n1952\twith the word ‘Now’ (this is a word US AD guidelines specify can be used to cue scene\n1953\tchanges) start a new scene.\n1954\t25\n1955\t\n1956\t\fPublished as a conference paper at COLM 2026\n1957\t\n1958\tParameter\n1959\t\n1960\tValue\n1961\t\n1962\tParameter\n1963\t\n1964\tValue\n1965\t\n1966\tBlock size\n1967\tLearning rate\n1968\tBatch size\n1969\tEpochs\n1970\tWeight decay\n1971\tWarmup ratio\n1972\t\n1973\t512\n1974\t1e-5\n1975\t64\n1976\t3\n1977\t0.01\n1978\t0.06\n1979\t\n1980\tBlock size\n1981\tLearning rate\n1982\tBatch size\n1983\tEpochs\n1984\tWeight decay\n1985\tWarmup ratio\n1986\t\n1987\t128\n1988\t1e-5\n1989\t32\n1990\t3\n1991\t0.01\n1992\t0.06\n1993\t\n1994\tTable 7: Hyperparameters for the scene\n1995\tsegmentation fine-tuning.\n1996\tP\n1997\t\n1998\tR\n1999\t\n2000\tBaseline\n2001\t\n2002\t—\n2003\t\n2004\t11.9±0.0 —\n2005\t\n2006\tRoBERTa sin.\n2007\t(base)\n2008\tdou.\n2009\t\n2010\t59.3±0.4 55.3±0.1 56.6±0.2\n2011\t73.2±1.1 45.4±0.3 55.5±0.4\n2012\t\n2013\tRoBERTa sin.\n2014\t(large) dou.\n2015\t\n2016\t65.0±0.2 55.8±0.1 59.5±0.2\n2017\t76.1±0.4 49.2±0.7 59.2±0.4\n2018\t\n2019\tTable 8: Hyperparameters for the token\n2020\tclassification AD splitting model.\n2021\t\n2022\tF1\n2023\tP\n2024\tBaseline\n2025\t\n2026\tR\n2027\t\n2028\tF1\n2029\t\n2030\t38.0±0.0 38.4±0.0 36.9±0.0\n2031\t\n2032\tRoBERTa (base) 64.9±0.1 66.2±0.0 63.7±0.1\n2033\tRoBERTa (large) 65.8±0.0 68.5±0.1 64.8±0.0\n2034\tTable 10: AD splitting performance\n2035\t(character-level F1 , precision P, recall R;\n2036\t10-fold CV, ± std). Task: classify whether\n2037\teach character ends a description element.\n2038\tBaseline: split at every comma.\n2039\t\n2040\tTable 9: Scene segmentation performance\n2041\t(binary F1 , precision P, recall R; 10-fold\n2042\tCV, ± std). sin.: per-version training;\n2043\tdou.: joint US/UK training for congruent\n2044\tboundaries. Baseline: segments starting\n2045\twith “Now” begin a new scene (P undefined as some tracks never use it).\n2046\t\n2047\tWe compare two formulations of the training task. The first is called ‘single’ and involves\n2048\tperforming training and inference on independent sliding windows of six consecutive\n2049\tsegments from individual AD transcripts. The second is called ‘double’ and involves\n2050\tperforming training and inference on paired sliding windows from US and UK transcripts\n2051\tto predict congruent boundaries across both. For ‘single’, we collect many samples of ‘[CLS]\n2052\t3 segments [SEP] 3 segments’ based on a sliding window of 6 segments. The learning task\n2053\tis binary classification on the CLS token of whether the [SEP] token represents a scene\n2054\tboundary or not. We collect all possible samples independently of each of the US and\n2055\tUK AD transcripts. ‘Double’ utilizes our prior that both the US and UK AD versions are\n2056\tdescribing the same movie scene changes. We formulate the task so that the model can only\n2057\tpredict congruent scene boundaries in both AD versions. The approach involves jointly\n2058\ttraining on sequences of US and UK AD using the following template: ‘[CLS] 3 segments\n2059\tof US AD [SEP] 3 segments of UK AD [SEP] 3 segments of US AD [SEP] 3 segments of UK\n2060\tAD’. The two versions may not begin narrating a scene at the same time. In fact, in 8.7% of\n2061\tscene boundaries in our data, after the first AD version starts a new scene, the other AD\n2062\tversion starts at least one new segment of the previous scene, before moving to the next\n2063\tscene. The boundary is fuzzy, and with this approach and two sliding windows, we can\n2064\ttreat this. We allow for the sliding windows to separate at the start of segment number\n2065\tfour (the potentially new scene) up to 10 seconds, or the next AD if there is none within\n2066\t10 seconds for one of the versions. The training task is now whether the second and third\n2067\t[SEP] tokens represent scene boundaries to the same next scene. To ensure the predicted\n2068\tscene boundaries are consistent, we use greedy inference, discarding predictions that are\n2069\tincompatible with earlier ones. We do not want our model to learn any signals present in\n2070\tour gold-standard AD transcripts, but not in automatic ASR transcripts. Hence we train on\n2071\ttranscripts from Speechmatics, segmented according to the gold-standard segmentation.\n2072\tThe full set of training hyperparameters is provided in Table 7.\n2073\tResults are in Table 9. The baseline approach achieves a recall score of 11.9, but precision\n2074\tis undefined since some of the movie ADs never use ‘now’. The ‘single’ and ‘double’\n2075\tapproaches with the RoBERTa-large model achieve similar F1 measures of 59.5 and 59.2,\n2076\t26\n2077\t\n2078\t\fPublished as a conference paper at COLM 2026\n2079\t\n2080\trespectively. Using the smaller RoBERTa-base model results in 56.6 and 55.5. Precision and\n2081\trecall metrics show that they are behaving differently: ‘double’ achieves higher precision,\n2082\tyet lower recall. This is intuitive, since the ‘double’ relies on a model’s prediction that a\n2083\tscene change is distinguishable in both transcripts. Since ‘double’ brings the additional\n2084\tbenefit of the scene boundaries guaranteed to be congruent in the two versions, we consider\n2085\tit best and use it. For the earlier video extract from The Girl with the Dragon Tattoo, this model\n2086\tsuccessfully segments into its three constituent scenes: at a tailor’s shop where the gift is\n2087\tpurchased, at Salander’s apartment where she writes a card, and the final street scene in\n2088\tFigure 1; as a result, the Vecalign-based alignment is able to successfully align each of these\n2089\tthree scenes with its corresponding scene in the screenplay.\n2090\tIncorporating video scene boundaries. The above procedure provides congruent scene\n2091\tlabels for each AD segment in our two AD versions. Re frame d contains AD for individual video scenes, which can also provide a signal of scene boundaries. In order to assign\n2092\tAD and subtitle segments to each video, we include all segments that are fully contained\n2093\tin the temporal span of the video. Furthermore, for the screenplay alignment to assign a\n2094\tparticular video a number of screenplay scenes, we must enforce that scenes do not span\n2095\tover the boundary of the start or end of a video – in such cases, we need to divide the scene\n2096\tlabels further. We also wish to transfer these scene labels to the available dialogue and\n2097\tSDH data. Since some videos in the training set have temporal overlap, we need a general\n2098\tre-assignment algorithm that caters to this.\n2099\tOur scene re-assignment algorithm meets all of the following requirements: (1) scene\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"28a31d0a-d593-4419-87c8-544579cdfc2c","uuid":"872606ac-b455-49f8-8ea5-32756c668f39","timestamp":"2026-08-11T12:12:50.421Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/reframed.txt","content":"\n101\n\n102\n\n103\n\nClip duration (s)\n\n(a) Accuracy of determining movie scene offset.\n\n(b) Accuracy of determining the speed difference.\n\nFigure 6: Scene-to-movie temporal alignment accuracy as a function of scene duration,\ncomparing cross-correlation (Soldan et al., 2022) against the RANSAC-based method of\nHan et al. (2024). Cross-correlation reaches perfect accuracy for scenes of ≥32 seconds,\nwhile the RANSAC method is reliable only at much longer durations; we therefore adopt\ncross-correlation in Re frame d.\nof the reference. Table 5 details the specific hyperparameters used for feature extraction and\nalignment.\nFigure 6 provides results. We can see that the success of the methods depends greatly on\nthe length of the scene to be aligned. The only approach to achieve perfect performance\nis using cross-correlation on scenes of at least 32 seconds. The RANSAC-based method\nperforms poorly without very long scene durations. Our experiment here also suggests why\nthe RANSAC method is inferior—it relies at its core on applying many cross-correlation\nto 3 second windows, and we have shown here that cross-correlation is very noisy at this\ntime-scale.\nIn Re frame d, we opt to use cross-correlation. In order to guarantee the highest possible\nquality assurance, we additionally require that all the offsets for a video from a given movie\nimpute an OLS regression line between the US and UK AD tracks with RMSE ≤ 1. We state\nthat we have high confidence in the alignment of the resulting videos based on the fact that\nit would be extremely unlikely for ≥5 independent alignment offsets to maintain linear\nconsistency across the entire movie if one or more was erroneous.\n\nD\n\nChallenge Set Annotation\n\nThe data in the Re frame d challenge set of full movies serves both as an evaluation for AD\ngeneration and as an evaluation material for further data collection steps that we perform.\nAnnotation for this set was performed manually.\nHigh-level Overview. There are 10 movies, and each movie is provided with 2-3 AD\ntranscripts. The movies are either 2023/2024 movies with award winning AD tracks (2/10),\nhave three independently-created AD versions (1/10), or are the same movies as in MADv3-eval (7/10). The exact frame that starts each new scene in every movie is labeled. Each\nAD segment is labeled with a label of the scene whose content it describes. The screenplays\nscenes are mapped to the movie scenes. Boundaries between sequences of scenes are\nobtained from the official movie release chapter boundaries. English SDH and English\ndialogue subtitles are from the official movie release.\nDetails. For each movie in our benchmark, we require the availability of independently\ncreated US and UK AD versions for use as multiple references. For seven out of the ten\nmovies in MAD-v3-eval, we were able to identify and purchase movies with the other AD\n22\n\n\fPublished as a conference paper at COLM 2026\n\nversion to the version in the previous data. We add two movies which received awards for\nthe quality of their AD tracks in the AD Awards gala and/or EGA Hermes Awards and also\nhave US and UK AD versions, namely Dune Part Two (2024) and Barbie (2023). We add a\nfinal movie for which we were able to obtain three independent AD tracks (two US versions,\none UK version), namely Priscilla (2023). The benchmark now contains movies that span\ntwo decades (2005–2024) and a wide range of genres.\nWe contracted the film post-production company to provide us with professional AD transcripts for each of the 21 (9 movies with two references, 1 movie with three references)\ntracks. An annotator manually determined if PAL–NTSC speedups and/or offsets are required between the versions and ensure that all versions are temporally aligned, determined\nthe scene boundaries in the movie and determined which AD segments correspond to\nwhich scene. The process was as follows: (1) using professional subtitling software, we\nlabeled the exact frame when each scene changes, following a strict definition of scene\nchanges as a change of location or time, (2) using these boundaries, the movie video, and\nthe timecoded AD, we label the scene to which each AD segment is describing content.\nScreenplays for 4/10 of the movies are provided by MovieSum (Saxena & Keller, 2024), and\nfor the remaining 6 we follow Saxena & Keller’s (2024) parsing method to obtain parsed\nscreenplays, which involves full manual review and correction. The annotator manually\nlabels the alignment between the movie scenes and the scenes in the screenplays from all\nten movies. There was no access to the screenplay to do the scene segmentation, as a result\none-to-many and many-to-one alignments are possible. Chapter boundaries are provided\ndirectly by our purchased versions of the movies, which we use as sequence boundaries.\nWe obtain professional dialogue subtitles by applying OCR (Tesseract 5.5.0) to the official\nmovie subtitles and manually reviewing (using Subtitle Edit software). We apply this to the\nEnglish SDH and English dialogue subtitles, or if only the former is available (4/10 cases)\nthen we manually edit the SDH version to remove text other than the dialogue subtitles.\nThe only potentially subjective task of the above is determining scene boundaries from the\nmovie. We do not perform any double-annotation and hence cannot report inter-annotator\nagreement on the task. One of the movies in our data is also provided as a part of MovieNet\n(Huang et al., 2020), which provides data that is the established benchmark for movie scene\nsegmentation. The MovieNet annotation of scene segmentation is crowd-sourced and based\non the decisions of an annotator (who likely has not watched the full movies) provided\naccess only to stills from neighbouring shots (determined via automatic shot detection). For\na movie in both datasets, Les Miserables (2012), we were surprised to find their annotation\nreports 228 scenes, while ours has only 113. We inspected their boundaries and found them\nto consistently oversegment, in particular in settings when neighbouring shots are visually\ndifferent but in fact just a different view of the same scene. As an independent reference, the\nscreenplay of this movie has 133 scenes (some of which were likely removed for the final\nversion).\n\nE\n\nNew Data Collection Techniques\n\nWorking with screenplays brings challenges, both in terms of parsing the PDFs available of\nthem and aligning them to the final movie.\nScreenplays are released as 100+ page PDFs, with a reasonably standard scene-segmented\nstructure that is displayed via indentations and font formatting. The task of parsing such\ndocuments into their structured contents has been addressed by prior work (Turetsky &\nDimitrova, 2004; Agarwal et al., 2014; Winer & Young, 2017). However, the task remains\nchallenging due to different formatting styles (Gil et al., 2011) and in practice several hours\nof human supervision is needed per movie for perfect parsing (Saxena & Keller, 2024). We\ndo not tackle this task and instead rely on the gold-standard machine-parsed screenplays\nfrom the MovieSum dataset (Saxena & Keller, 2024).\nHowever, we found current alignment methods to be insufficient for our purposes. In\nour approach to screenplay alignment, we define the task at the scene-level as follows:\ngiven S = {si }iS=1 scenes from a machine-parseable screenplay and the dialogue and AD\n23\n\n\fPublished as a conference paper at COLM 2026\n\nfrom M = {m j } jM=1 scenes in the final movie, compute the alignment A ⊆ S × M. The\nscreenplay’s structured format provides exact segmentation into the S scenes, but scene\nsegmentation is not available for the final movie. Our insight here is that AD will frequently\ncue movie scene changes; this is a requirement stipulated in both American and British AD\nguidelines. If we can detect such cues, then we have a high-precision and scalable method\nof determining movie scene boundaries.\nE.1\n\nMovie-Screenplay Alignment\n\nEvaluation and Baselines. Our evaluation metric for movie-screenplay alignment is\nweighted F1 score, where the weights are based on the lengths of the scenes in the movie. We\nwill compare our methods against the previous state-of-the-art: aligning a movie’s dialogue\nsubtitles with dialogue in the movie’s screenplay by computing a dynamic time warping\n(DTW) over word tokens. In this baseline, we assign any screenplay dialogue word with an\nexact match to a movie dialogue word the scene the movie word is from.\nDTW over word matches is frequently applied for the task of movie-screenplay alignment\n(Everingham et al., 2006; Sivic et al., 2009; Bouritsas et al., 2018; Papalampidi et al., 2021).\nNevertheless, results from Lambert et al. (2013) suggest that DTW may not be the ideal\nalgorithm, because it favours on-diagonal matches in the alignment. This is a particular\nproblem for screenplay drafts before a post-production or even shooting version. Lambert et al. (2013) propose to upweight the score that results from matches; this is akin to\nminimizing edit distance, which we will employ as a second baseline.2\nVecalign-based Alignment. In our approach, we compute embeddings for each scene in\nthe movie and each scene in the screenplay, and then use dynamic programming to align\nthe two. We additionally treat the potential for a screenplay scene to have been removed\nat production, or for a new scene to be added at production. Thompson & Koehn (2019)\nprovide methods designed for compiling machine translation corpora that can be of use\nhere, which they call Vecalign. In particular they define a cost in the alignment algorithm\nwhich allows scenes to be skipped in either the movie or the screenplay, without alignment.\nWe use a fixed skip cost cskip = 0.4. Vecalign also treats one-to-many and many-to-one\nalignments by allowing for merge operations between contiguous sequences of scenes. We\nuse mean-pooling as the merge operation. We allow for alignment blocks of up to 10 scenes.\nVecalign also allows for, albeit discourages in the cost function, many-to-many alignments;\nwe disallow these via search constraints so that the final alignment is strictly monotonic\nover the movie scenes. Vecalign also incorporates methods that reduce the alignment time\ncomplexity from quadratic to linear via a recursive approximation; we do not apply these\nmethods and optimize over the full alignment.\nThe cost c(s, m) of aligning a candidate block of screenplay scenes s ⊆ S to a candidate\nblock of movie scenes m ⊆ M is defined as:\n\u0001\n1 − cos(s̄, m̄) · |s| · |m|\nc(s, m) = 1\n(1)\n\u0001\n\u0001\nM\nS\n1\n2M ∑ j=1 1 − cos( s̄, m j ) + 2S ∑i =1 1 − cos( si , m̄ )\nwhere boldface denotes scene embeddings, bar notation denotes embeddings after meanpooling, and | · | denotes the number of scenes in a block. The numerator scales the similarity\ncost by the number of scenes in the block alignment, and the denominator normalizes by\nthe mean similarity between the candidate block and all scenes.\nWith initialization D (0, 0) = 0, D (i, 0) = i · cskip , and D (0, j) = j · cskip , the dynamic\nprogramming recurrence relation is as follows:\n\nD (i − 1, j) + cskip\n(skip screenplay scene)\n\n\n\nD\n(\ni,\nj\n−\n1\n)\n+\nc\n(skip movie scene)\nskip\nD (i, j) = min\nh\n\u0001i\n\n\n(block match)\n min D (i − |s|, j − |m|) + c Si−|s|:i , M j−|m|:j\n| s |,| m |\n\n2 We use the implementation from https://github.com/jitsi/jiwer\n\n24\n\n\fPublished as a conference paper at COLM 2026\n\nModel\n\nP\n\nR\n\nF1\n\n—\n—\n\n63.7\n66.4\n\n62.4\n63.1\n\n62.3\n63.8\n\ndialogue\nSDH\nUK AD\nUS AD\nUK AD + US AD\ndialogue + UK AD\nSDH + UK AD\ndialogue + US AD\nSDH + US AD\ndialogue + UK AD + US AD\n\nQwen3 E 8B\nQwen3 E 8B\nQwen3 E 8B\nQwen3 E 8B\nQwen3 E 8B\nQwen3 E 8B\nQwen3 E 8B\nQwen3 E 8B\nQwen3 E 8B\nQwen3 E 8B\n\n81.4\n82.8\n76.9\n77.0\n76.9\n82.1\n83.6\n84.7\n85.1\n83.1\n\n53.4\n58.5\n67.1\n70.0\n72.8\n73.7\n73.1\n73.9\n73.9\n77.4\n\n64.2\n68.0\n71.3\n73.0\n74.5\n77.3\n77.5\n78.6\n78.7\n79.9\n\nSDH + UK AD + US AD\nSDH + UK AD + US AD\nSDH + UK AD + US AD\n\nQwen3 E 0.6B\nQwen3 E 4B\nQwen3 E 8B\n\n87.5\n85.3\n84.9\n\n62.4\n73.8\n77.7\n\n71.8\n78.8\n80.9\n\nMethod\n\nData\n\nText-based DTW\nText-based MED\n\ndialogue\ndialogue\n\nVecalign\nVecalign\nVecalign\nVecalign\nVecalign\nVecalign\nVecalign\nVecalign\nVecalign\nVecalign\nVecalign\nVecalign\nVecalign\n\nTable 6: Screenplay alignment results. DTW = dynamic time warping, MED = minimum\nedit distance. Text-based baselines are in first block, ablation results are in second block,\nand best results based on embedding models of three sizes are in final block.\n\nwhere the search space is constrained such that min(|s|, |m|) = 1 and |s| + |m| ≤ 10. The\nfinal alignment A is then recovered by backtracking along the minimal-cost path from\nD (S, M ) to D (0, 0) and collecting the matched scenes.\nEmbeddings. For the movies, we will define the embeddings based on a time-sorted\nconcatenation of the subtitles (SDH or dialogue-only) and/or AD (UK and/or US versions)\nin each scene in our gold-standard annotation; for the screenplay we define the embedding\nbased on the dialogue and scene descriptions in each scene in the order that they appear.\nEmbeddings are computed according to Zhang et al. (2025).\nResults. Results are in Table 6. The word-based DTW baseline achieves F1 = 62.3, and the\nminimum edit distance variant achieves F1 = 63.8. Performance with Vecalign applied to\nthe same dialogue is comparable at F1 = 64.2. Using instead SDH leads to an improved F1 =\n68.0 (+3.8), demonstrating that the non-verbal audio captions contain useful information\nfor the alignment. We can outperform these dialogue-based alignment approaches using\nonly one of UK or US AD (F1 = 71.3 and F1 = 73.0), demonstrating that AD also contains\nuseful information for the alignment. Using both AD versions results in better performance\nthan using only one (F1 = 74.5, +3.2 and +1.5 for AD versions). (Table 6 also gives results\nfor other combinations of input data.) Our best results (F1 =80.9) are based on using SDH\nand both AD versions with Vecalign. In our experiments so far, we have used the Qwen3\nEmbedding 8B model for computing the embeddings. Using the 4B or 0.6B variants of this\nmodel leads to worse performance (F1 =78.8 and F1 =71.8, respectively). We apply this best\nalignment pipeline to all movies with screenplays in Re frame d.\nE.2\n\nMovie Scene Segmentation from AD\n\nAD scene segmentation. We detect scene change cues in AD by finetuning RoBERTalarge (Liu et al., 2019) on our manually annotated data. The task is for a model to classify\neach segment of AD as starting a new scene or not, given the context of surrounding AD\nsegments. Our evaluation metric is binary F1 score. We perform 10-fold cross-validation\nand report the macro-average. Our baseline will assume that all AD segments that begin\nwith the word ‘Now’ (this is a word US AD guidelines specify can be used to cue scene\nchanges) start a new scene.\n25\n\n\fPublished as a conference paper at COLM 2026\n\nParameter\n\nValue\n\nParameter\n\nValue\n\nBlock size\nLearning rate\nBatch size\nEpochs\nWeight decay\nWarmup ratio\n\n512\n1e-5\n64\n3\n0.01\n0.06\n\nBlock size\nLearning rate\nBatch size\nEpochs\nWeight decay\nWarmup ratio\n\n128\n1e-5\n32\n3\n0.01\n0.06\n\nTable 7: Hyperparameters for the scene\nsegmentation fine-tuning.\nP\n\nR\n\nBaseline\n\n—\n\n11.9±0.0 —\n\nRoBERTa sin.\n(base)\ndou.\n\n59.3±0.4 55.3±0.1 56.6±0.2\n73.2±1.1 45.4±0.3 55.5±0.4\n\nRoBERTa sin.\n(large) dou.\n\n65.0±0.2 55.8±0.1 59.5±0.2\n76.1±0.4 49.2±0.7 59.2±0.4\n\nTable 8: Hyperparameters for the token\nclassification AD splitting model.\n\nF1\nP\nBaseline\n\nR\n\nF1\n\n38.0±0.0 38.4±0.0 36.9±0.0\n\nRoBERTa (base) 64.9±0.1 66.2±0.0 63.7±0.1\nRoBERTa (large) 65.8±0.0 68.5±0.1 64.8±0.0\nTable 10: AD splitting performance\n(character-level F1 , precision P, recall R;\n10-fold CV, ± std). Task: classify whether\neach character ends a description element.\nBaseline: split at every comma.\n\nTable 9: Scene segmentation performance\n(binary F1 , precision P, recall R; 10-fold\nCV, ± std). sin.: per-version training;\ndou.: joint US/UK training for congruent\nboundaries. Baseline: segments starting\nwith “Now” begin a new scene (P undefined as some tracks never use it).\n\nWe compare two formulations of the training task. The first is called ‘single’ and involves\nperforming training and inference on independent sliding windows of six consecutive\nsegments from individual AD transcripts. The second is called ‘double’ and involves\nperforming training and inference on paired sliding windows from US and UK transcripts\nto predict congruent boundaries across both. For ‘single’, we collect many samples of ‘[CLS]\n3 segments [SEP] 3 segments’ based on a sliding window of 6 segments. The learning task\nis binary classification on the CLS token of whether the [SEP] token represents a scene\nboundary or not. We collect all possible samples independently of each of the US and\nUK AD transcripts. ‘Double’ utilizes our prior that both the US and UK AD versions are\ndescribing the same movie scene changes. We formulate the task so that the model can only\npredict congruent scene boundaries in both AD versions. The approach involves jointly\ntraining on sequences of US and UK AD using the following template: ‘[CLS] 3 segments\nof US AD [SEP] 3 segments of UK AD [SEP] 3 segments of US AD [SEP] 3 segments of UK\nAD’. The two versions may not begin narrating a scene at the same time. In fact, in 8.7% of\nscene boundaries in our data, after the first AD version starts a new scene, the other AD\nversion starts at least one new segment of the previous scene, before moving to the next\nscene. The boundary is fuzzy, and with this approach and two sliding windows, we can\ntreat this. We allow for the sliding windows to separate at the start of segment number\nfour (the potentially new scene) up to 10 seconds, or the next AD if there is none within\n10 seconds for one of the versions. The training task is now whether the second and third\n[SEP] tokens represent scene boundaries to the same next scene. To ensure the predicted\nscene boundaries are consistent, we use greedy inference, discarding predictions that are\nincompatible with earlier ones. We do not want our model to learn any signals present in\nour gold-standard AD transcripts, but not in automatic ASR transcripts. Hence we train on\ntranscripts from Speechmatics, segmented according to the gold-standard segmentation.\nThe full set of training hyperparameters is provided in Table 7.\nResults are in Table 9. The baseline approach achieves a recall score of 11.9, but precision\nis undefined since some of the movie ADs never use ‘now’. The ‘single’ and ‘double’\napproaches with the RoBERTa-large model achieve similar F1 measures of 59.5 and 59.2,\n26\n\n\fPublished as a conference paper at COLM 2026\n\nrespectively. Using the smaller RoBERTa-base model results in 56.6 and 55.5. Precision and\nrecall metrics show that they are behaving differently: ‘double’ achieves higher precision,\nyet lower recall. This is intuitive, since the ‘double’ relies on a model’s prediction that a\nscene change is distinguishable in both transcripts. Since ‘double’ brings the additional\nbenefit of the scene boundaries guaranteed to be congruent in the two versions, we consider\nit best and use it. For the earlier video extract from The Girl with the Dragon Tattoo, this model\nsuccessfully segments into its three constituent scenes: at a tailor’s shop where the gift is\npurchased, at Salander’s apartment where she writes a card, and the final street scene in\nFigure 1; as a result, the Vecalign-based alignment is able to successfully align each of these\nthree scenes with its corresponding scene in the screenplay.\nIncorporating video scene boundaries. The above procedure provides congruent scene\nlabels for each AD segment in our two AD versions. Re frame d contains AD for individual video scenes, which can also provide a signal of scene boundaries. In order to assign\nAD and subtitle segments to each video, we include all segments that are fully contained\nin the temporal span of the video. Furthermore, for the screenplay alignment to assign a\nparticular video a number of screenplay scenes, we must enforce that scenes do not span\nover the boundary of the start or end of a video – in such cases, we need to divide the scene\nlabels further. We also wish to transfer these scene labels to the available dialogue and\nSDH data. Since some videos in the training set have temporal overlap, we need a general\nre-assignment algorithm that caters to this.\nOur scene re-assignment algorithm meets all of the following requirements: (1) scene","numLines":500,"startLine":1600,"totalLines":3998}}}
