{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-96afe5b81588be96","type":"tool_result","content":"2100\tboundaries according to the AD classifier are retained; (2) temporal scene boundaries are\n2101\tcreated for the start and end of every video; and (3) the data associated with each video can\n2102\tbe reconstructed by concatenating the data associated with a particular set of scene labels.\n2103\tThis is achieved by constructing an intermediate scene label for every segment before\n2104\tmapping these labels to final shared scene identifiers. Each AD segment is initialized\n2105\twith the scene label assigned by the AD classifier. We then append the identifiers of all\n2106\tvideos in which the segment is fully contained. To distinguish different positions relative\n2107\tto video boundaries, we partition the movie timeline at every video start and end time.\n2108\tEach resulting interval is associated with the set of videos active during that interval, which\n2109\tmay be none. For each segment, we assign the interval whose active video set matches the\n2110\tsegment’s fully contained video set, when such an interval is unique. If no unique matching\n2111\tinterval exists, the segment is assigned a boundary-spanning interval covering the relevant\n2112\tvideo-boundary regions. Thus, each AD segment receives an intermediate label consisting\n2113\tof its classifier scene, its fully contained video set, and its position relative to the global\n2114\tvideo-boundary partition.\n2115\tFor subtitle and SDH segments fully contained in videos, we infer the classifier scene from\n2116\tAD segments in the same video. When multiple AD scenes occur in the video, the subtitle\n2117\tis assigned to the one with greatest temporal overlap. For subtitle and SDH segments\n2118\toutside videos, we infer the classifier scene from global intervals derived from the first\n2119\toccurrence of each AD classifier scene across both AD versions, partitioning the full movie\n2120\ttimeline from zero to the latest segment or video end. The classification again uses greatest\n2121\ttemporal overlap. If a video contains subtitle or SDH segments but no AD segments, we\n2122\tadd a subtitle-only scene label for that video setting. Finally, all intermediate labels across\n2123\tthe two AD versions, subtitles, and SDH subtitles are mapped to shared consecutive scene\n2124\tidentifiers in order of their first occurrence. For the screenplay alignment, each movie scene\n2125\tof length less than 10 seconds and not a part of any video is excluded from the alignment.\n2126\tE.3\n2127\t\n2128\tAD Splitting\n2129\t\n2130\tASR systems output transcripts at the sentence level, which are potentially long (the longest\n2131\tin our data is 74 words). To be consistent with our evaluation data, we would like to segment\n2132\ttranscribed AD sentences into the separate elements they describe. This task is only defined\n2133\tfully with access to the movie visuals, but we expect that reasonable performance is possible\n2134\tusing only the textual sentences as input. Our operationalisation draws inspiration from\n2135\tFrohmann et al. (2024), who formulate the task of text segmentation as classifying whether\n2136\t27\n2137\t\n2138\t\fPublished as a conference paper at COLM 2026\n2139\t\n2140\teach token in a text ends a segment or not. We create training data by splitting Speechmatics\n2141\tAD transcript sentences according to the segmentation in our gold standard. This results\n2142\tin 16,496 sentences split into 21,675 segments (26.45% of sentences have more than one\n2143\tsegment). We evaluate using character-level F1 on segment-ending characters. Our baseline\n2144\tcreates an AD split at every comma. Table 8 provides the full set of hyperparameters used.\n2145\tResults are in Table 10. The comma baseline achieves F1 =36.9, while our best model achieves\n2146\tF1 =64.8. Evidently the task is not defined perfectly, likely in part because of our text-only\n2147\tformulation, but we deem this satisfactory performance. Manual inspection shows that\n2148\tthe resulting model is quite robust to punctuation and complex syntax. For example, the\n2149\tAD ‘His right hand forms a cannon, which he aims down the corridor before inserting\n2150\ta probe into the mainframe terminal at a radio telescope array, three dishes shift their\n2151\tpositions.’ becomes 4 segments (starting ‘His’, ‘which’, ‘before’ and ‘at’). This example also\n2152\tdemonstrates how preprocessing our data with this segmentation model is an important\n2153\tpre-processing step before running the scene segmentation model, because the segment\n2154\tbeginning ‘at’ actually starts a new scene.\n2155\t\n2156\tF\n2157\t\n2158\tMore Details of AD Transcription Experiments\n2159\t\n2160\tWe evaluate ASR-only performance using gold-standard diarization; metrics include segment error rate (SER) and word error rate (WER) (which is derived from substitution rate\n2161\t(SR), deletion rate (DR) and insertion rate (IR)). Binary diarization error rate (DER) gives\n2162\tdiarization performance; it is derived from miss rate (MR) and false alarm rate (FAR). We\n2163\tevaluate the error rate of timestamps against collars of α seconds around the reference timestamps. Character names in the reference are manually identified and character name error\n2164\trate is a micro average over reference characters spelt correctly. Finally, overall ASR metrics\n2165\tshow end-to-end performance, i.e. after automatic diarization. All text is pre-processed\n2166\taccording to Radford et al. (2023).3\n2167\tWe can also evaluate the quality of the MAD-v2-eval transcripts against our new reference.\n2168\tThe models we compare are as follows:\n2169\t• The system used for AD transcription in MAD-v2-train and CMD-AD datasets\n2170\t(Han et al., 2023a; 2024): WhisperX. It is an open-source system that combines\n2171\tmultiple open-source projects. We also tried customizing the system to use\n2172\tgpt-4o-transcribe for the ASR.\n2173\t• The system used for AD transcription in the MAD-v1-train dataset (Soldan et al.,\n2174\t2022): Microsoft Batch API system. Since our data consists of both American\n2175\tEnglish and British English accents. We investigated variants with and without continuous language identification between British and American English; in summary,\n2176\tincluding this language identification brings substantial performance gains. We\n2177\tshow results including using this language identification.\n2178\t• A system previously not applied to the task, but generally-reported good performance: Speechmatics Batch API standard and enhanced systems, global English.\n2179\tCharacters play a central role in movie narratives, and hence it is vital that they are transcribed correctly. We apply pre-existing methods for guiding ASR models to spell particular\n2180\twords correctly, which for us is character names. We collect lists of all character names\n2181\tin movie credits, except those that are non-unique (e.g. ‘Citizen’) or ending in a numeric\n2182\tcharacter (e.g. ‘Citizen 1’).4\n2183\t• Whisper: we override Whisper’s in-context tokens with a script containing all\n2184\tcharacter names. We compared three different script templates and proceeded with\n2185\tthe best one: a comma separated list of the character names, which outperformed\n2186\tspecifying the name of the movie and a template more realistic of a real conversation.\n2187\t• Microsoft: vocabulary adaptation is not available in Batch API.\n2188\t3 https://github.com/openai/whisper/tree/main/whisper/normalizers\n2189\t4 These are available on ‘fullcredits’ pages on IMDb.\n2190\t\n2191\t28\n2192\t\n2193\t\fPublished as a conference paper at COLM 2026\n2194\t\n2195\tASR\n2196\tSER WER\n2197\tlarge-v3-turbo\n2198\t23.5\n2199\tgpt-4o-transcribe 49.0\n2200\tlarge-v3\n2201\t23.5\n2202\tlarge-v2\n2203\t27.3\n2204\tlarge\n2205\t23.5\n2206\tmedium\n2207\t27.4\n2208\t