splits, we find matches\n3289\tfor grams up to length 17 tokens, demonstrating contamination. The contamination is\n3290\tnoticeably higher for the validation set as compared to the test set (which contains newer\n3291\tmovies from 2023–2025).\n3292\t\n3293\tM\n3294\t\n3295\tLLM AD Generation\n3296\t\n3297\tM.1\n3298\t\n3299\tPrompt\n3300\t\n3301\tLLM Prompt\n3302\tTask description:\n3303\tYou are provided with the video (no audio) and dialogue subtitles for a full movie.\n3304\tYour task is to generate an audio description script for one part of the movie.\n3305\tAudio description is a verbal narration of key visual content in a video.\n3306\tDescriptions must be inserted into gaps between dialogue.\n3307\tOutput format:\n3308\tReturn exactly one valid JSON object and no other text. Each key represents a gap\n3309\tbetween dialogue. Each value is an array of description objects. Each description\n3310\tobject contains a textual description and timestamps for when it should be\n3311\tnarrated. The following illustrates the required structure:\n3312\t{\"1st DIALOGUE GAP START --> DIALOGUE GAP END\": [{\"text\": \"TEXT\", \"start\":\n3313\t\"START\", \"end\": \"END\"}, {\"text\": \"TEXT\", \"start\": \"START\", \"end\": \"END\"}, {\"text\":\n3314\t\"TEXT\", \"start\": \"START\", \"end\": \"END\"}], \"2nd DIALOGUE GAP START --> DIALOGUE GAP\n3315\tEND\": [{\"text\": \"TEXT\", \"start\": \"START\", \"end\": \"END\"}]}\n3316\t- DIALOGUE GAP START --> DIALOGUE GAP END takes the form \"TIMESTAMP_FORMAT -->\n3317\tTIMESTAMP_FORMAT\" and is the temporal interval when descriptions can be inserted.\n3318\t- TEXT is a textual description.\n3319\t- START and END are timestamps with format \"TIMESTAMP_FORMAT\".\n3320\tRules:\n3321\t\n3322\t37\n3323\t\n3324\t\fPublished as a conference paper at COLM 2026\n3325\t\n3326\t- Each value must be an array of zero, one, or more description objects, each\n3327\tcontaining exactly the keys \"text\", \"start\", and \"end\".\n3328\t- For each description object:\n3329\t- TEXT is one sentence or shorter\n3330\t- # words in TEXT approx 3 x (END - START) in seconds\n3331\t- START >= DIALOGUE GAP START\n3332\t- END <= DIALOGUE GAP END\n3333\t- END > START\n3334\t- Description objects must never overlap in time\n3335\tAudio description script for prior parts:\n3336\tINSERT_PRIOR_AUDIO_DESCRIPTION\n3337\tRequired part:\n3338\tYou should now generate an audio description script for the part of the movie that\n3339\tstarts at PART_START_TIME and ends at PART_END_TIME.\n3340\tGaps between dialogue in the required part of the movie:\n3341\tThe following is the required output template, with the dialogue gaps in the\n3342\trequired part of the movie included as keys:\n3343\tOUTPUT_TEMPLATE\n3344\tGenerate the JSON with the values filled in. Your JSON must include all keys in\n3345\tthe template above.\n3346\t\n3347\tFor chunked video inputs, we adapt the prompt to state that the supplied video is one part\n3348\tof the movie rather than the full movie, remove the absolute start and end times of the\n3349\trequired part, and express the subtitles, dialogue gaps, and generated timestamps relative\n3350\tto the beginning of the chunk.\n3351\tVideo (1FPS, no audio) is the first part of every prompt, followed by dialogue subtitles.\n3352\tDialogue subtitles take the form START --> END TEXT. For dialogue gaps, we include those\n3353\tthat are at least one second in duration. Timestamps are formatted according to model\n3354\tvideo preprocessing (MM:SS for Gemini and <S.S seconds> for Qwen). For chunked video\n3355\tgeneration, relative output timestamps are converted to absolute movie times.\n3356\tIterative prompts include previously generated descriptions that were successfully parsed\n3357\tand validated against the prompt instructions:\n3358\t• Malformed JSON (between first and last braces) and responses terminating due to\n3359\tmaximum output length are treated as empty output.\n3360\t• We retain only keys that match an eligible dialogue gap and whose values are arrays.\n3361\tFor Qwen, we additionally accept otherwise exact gap keys from which the angle\n3362\tbrackets around timestamps are missing.\n3363\t• We retain only description objects containing the required text, start, and end\n3364\tfields, with nonempty textual descriptions and parseable timestamps.\n3365\t• We require each segment to have a strictly positive duration and to be fully contained within the dialogue gap identified by its key.\n3366\t• Within each gap, we remove duplicate segments, order the remaining segments by\n3367\tstart and end time, and discard any segment that overlaps the preceding retained\n3368\tsegment.\n3369\tFor chunked video input with iterative generation on our challenge set, timestamps risk\n3370\tbecoming ambiguous. From the second ten-minute chunk onwards, timestamps in the\n3371\taccumulated AD script refer to the movie timeline, while those in the required output refer\n3372\tto the current chunk’s timeline. To address this ambiguity, we compared providing the AD\n3373\tscript for prior chunks as prose text, without timestamps, against providing it in the script\n3374\t38\n3375\t\n3376\t\fPublished as a conference paper at COLM 2026\n3377\t\n3378\tformat. Results are in Table 14. The two prompting approaches are largely indistinguishable,\n3379\twith small improvements in the QA-based metrics for the prose variant, which we therefore\n3380\tadopt.\n3381\tCIDEr\n3382\tQwen prose\n3383\tQwen script\n3384\tGemini prose\n3385\tGemini script\n3386\t\n3387\tMETEOR\n3388\t\n3389\t13.2 (0–2)\n3390\t13.3 (0–2)\n3391\t19.0 (2–0)\n3392\t19.6 (2–0)\n3393\t\n3394\t8.1 (0–0)\n3395\t7.8 (0–0)\n3396\t8.2 (0–0)\n3397\t8.2 (0–0)\n3398\t\n3399\tSODA-M\n3400\t7.4 (0–2)\n3401\t7.4 (0–2)\n3402\t7.9 (2–0)\n3403\t7.9 (2–0)\n3404\t\n3405\tSODA-T\n3406\t38.4 (2–0)\n3407\t35.4 (0–1)\n3408\t36.0 (0–0)\n3409\t34.6 (0–1)\n3410\t\n3411\tQEval\n3412\t\n3413\tQEval-T\n3414\t\n3415\t43.9 (3–0)\n3416\t43.4 (2–1)